    51	## 3. Architecture thesis
    52	
    53	### 3.1 T1: ledger plus idiomatic entity projection, written in one atomic step
    54	
    55	The ledger stays the canonical record: exact values, both clocks, assertion identity, retractions,
    56	receipts, journal and claims. Each engine adds a projection in its own idiom, derived from a
    57	content-addressed **projection spec** (EntityTypes and attributes mapped to physical names and
    58	types), and written in the same atomic step as the ledger.
    59	
    60	```
    61	                          Triplex TypeScript API
    62	   +------------------------------------------------------------------+
    63	   | Repository<Model> (typed from EntityType): at(basis) get getMany |
    64	   | find({where, include, limit, cursor}) timeline                   |
    65	   | Writer -> WritePlan -> commit(plans, {actor, commandId})          |
    66	   | native.xtql`` native.sql`` native.sparql`` + declared contract    |
    67	   +--------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    68	                                    |
    69	         current-basis reads, raw   |   exact temporal reads, history,
    70	         queries, index lookups     |   receipts, journal, claims
    71	                     v              |              v
    72	   +--------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    73	   |        ENTITY PROJECTION         |       ASSERTION LEDGER          |
    74	   |                                  |                                 |
    75	   | XTDB                             | XTDB                            |
    76	   |  table per EntityType            |  tx_assertions, tx_meta,        |
    77	   |   _id = entity id                |  tx_claims, tx_attempts         |
    78	   |   ns$attr column per attribute,  |  (existing), plus an entity-    |
    79	   |   one declared type each         |  keyed access path so history   |
    80	   |   native valid time = business   |  reads avoid a ledger scan      |
    81	   |   time                           |                                 |
    82	   |  index tables keyed by value:    | QLever                          |
    83	   |   unique attrs, attr-value,      |  compact assertion nodes        |
    84	   |   reverse refs                   |  (target about 7 triples),      |
    85	   |                                  |  field predicates in their own  |
    86	   | QLever                           |  namespace, order keys for text |
    87	   |  current graph per scope         |  and numbers                    |
    88	   |   <e> rdf:type <Type>            |                                 |
    89	   |   <e> <attr> typed literal       |                                 |
    90	   |   native types only where exact  |                                 |
    91	   +----------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    92	     XTDB: one transaction for ledger, projection and index tables.
    93	     QLever: roll-forward protocol (ledger, verify, watermark, projection).
    94	```
    95	
    96	**Why T1:**
    97	
    98	| Option | Raw queries make sense | Exact semantics | Meets 100m-fact scale | Role |
    99	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   100	| A0: ledger only (status quo) | no: EAV pivots, reified joins | yes | no (scans in XTDB; 3 to 5.7B triples in QLever) | control baseline |
   101	| A1: projection only | yes | no: loses assertion identity and exact recorded cuts | maybe | rejected after research |
   102	| T1: ledger plus projection | yes | yes | target | primary |
   103	
   104	### 3.2 Lane X physical design
   105	
   106	1. **Every hot read resolves through `_id`.** Projection tables use `_id` = entity id. Lookups by
   107	   value go through maintained index tables whose `_id` is a canonical value key: unique-attribute
   108	   tables (also used for uniqueness enforcement in place of a 1.8 s column-scan `ASSERT`),
   109	   attribute-value tables, and reverse-reference tables (`_id` = target entity id). R7 measures the
   110	   shapes before this is fixed.
   111	2. **Projection writer, two rivals.** X3a patches only the affected attribute segments
   112	   (`PATCH`/`UPDATE ... FOR PORTION OF VALID_TIME`, values computed from the ledger), so cost grows
   113	   with the slices touched. X3b rewrites the entity's whole valid-time timeline, which is simplest
   114	   but adds O(history) row versions per write. The scale ladder decides.
   115	3. **Correctness oracle for the projection.** The projection must equal a pure function of the
   116	   ledger at the newest position. The entity suite recomputes it from the ledger and compares.
   117	4. **Hypothesis H-X-CUT (exact recorded cut without setting system time).** Every row written in
   118	   one XTDB transaction shares that transaction's `_system_from`, and system time strictly increases
   119	   per transaction. So commit position P maps exactly to the `_system_from` of the `tx_meta` row
   120	   version written in P's transaction, and a Triplex read at recorded cut P can run as
   121	   `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` that instant. This must be proven, including after aborts, restarts and
   122	   same-millisecond commits, before any read depends on it.
   123	5. **Types.** Each projection column has exactly one declared type; int64 is always BIGINT;
   124	   mixed-type columns are forbidden because they reach Node as JSON and lose integer precision.
   125	   Valid-time coordinates that cannot be represented exactly in microseconds route that entity's
   126	   reads to the ledger.
   127	6. **Card-one conflicts.** When the ledger holds two live, overlapping values for a cardinality-one
   128	   attribute (possible when enforcement is off), the projection writes NULL and records the attribute
   129	   in a `_triplex_conflicts` column; the repository surfaces a typed conflict error. Raw queries see
   130	   the NULL and the marker, never an arbitrary pick.
   131	7. **Engine bugs to route around.** `SUM` over BIGINT wraps silently; XTQL `union-all` cannot be
   132	   planned; window functions hit `AbstractMethodError`. Native helpers reject or rewrite these shapes.
   133	
   134	### 3.3 Lane Q physical design
   135	
   136	1. **Compact ledger.** Assertion nodes shrink from about 30 triples to about 7: entity, attribute,
   137	   value (exact lexical form), valid-from key, optional valid-to key, transaction node, plus a text
   138	   order key where the attribute is orderable. Recorded time, actor and command live once on the
   139	   transaction node. The journal is derived from assertion nodes (created or retracted per position)
   140	   instead of stored change JSON, if R9 confirms core can accept a native journal.
   141	2. **Current graph.** Plain triples (`rdf:type`, attribute predicates, typed literals only inside the
   142	   engine's exact ranges) for facts live at the newest position and valid at the graph's declared
   143	   valid instant. Plain attribute-predicate triples exist nowhere else, so raw queries over QLever's
   144	   union default graph never see historical values. A boundary maintainer applies due changes when
   145	   validity starts or ends; every read reports the graph's position and valid instant.
   146	3. **Rival H-Q3b.** Plain triples inside per-assertion named graphs (R3's preference, exact at any
   147	   basis). Kept as a measured rival because 100m named graphs are an unmeasured cost and the union
   148	   default graph would expose historical values to raw queries.
   149	4. **Roll-forward write protocol.** One operation per request; ledger triples applied idempotently;
   150	   read-back verification; a position watermark published only after verification; readers bound
   151	   every ledger read by the watermark; a reconciler re-applies positions above the watermark after a
   152	   crash; the projection follows its own watermark. Delta is bounded by online `rebuild-index`
   153	   (first trigger near 1M delta triples). R8 measures each step.
   154	5. **Semantic fixes.** Text order keys (R5, proven). `ctx.notExists`/`ctx.exists` helpers that
   155	   re-bind correlated variables plus a static check (R5, verified). `query-planning-budget` set low
   156	   after costing it on the native packet.
   157	
   158	### 3.4 T2: fix semantic gaps at the cheapest correct layer
   159	
   160	Ranked order for every counterexample, cheapest first: authoring rewrite in our helpers with written
   161	equivalence conditions; a maintained key whose order the pinned engine provably respects; engine
   162	configuration; engine repair as a separately named patched image; TypeScript evaluation over a
   163	bounded set, counted, never by re-sorting a returned page. No casefolding, no page re-sorting, no
   164	oracle rewrites.
   165	
   166	### 3.5 T3: scale is climbed rung by rung
   167	
   168	Rungs are 10k, 100k, 1m and 10m entities, about 10 active facts per entity in the representative
   169	profile. A rung opens only after the previous rung passes its correctness subsample. Each rung
   170	reports bytes per entity, ingest rate, query-mix latency (p50, p95, p99), write amplification,
   171	rebuild or compaction time and peak memory. Disk is the first limit above 100k entities, so every
   172	rung starts with a disk forecast.
