     1	# triplex-db-2 dev-loop plan (v1-pre, before adversarial review)
     2	
     3	Status: v1-pre, 2026-11-07. Folds in research wave R1 to R6 (`lab/research/`). Follow-up research R7
     4	(XTDB indexing and ingest), R8 (QLever write lifecycle) and R9 (core plumbing) is running. The
     5	changelog at the bottom records what changed between versions and why.
     6	
     7	## 1. Mission and done condition
     8	
     9	**Mission.** Build an efficient triple store where Triplex is the TypeScript interface, used almost
    10	like an ORM, over two engines whose native query languages we maintain:
    11	
    12	| Lane | Engine (pinned) | Native language | Named alternative |
    13	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
    14	| X | XTDB 2.2.0, image digest `a6a42650...` | XTQL (primary) | XTDB SQL |
    15	| Q | QLever 0.6.0, image digest `37d5ede1...` | SPARQL | none |
    16	
    17	**Raw queries must make sense.** A developer who knows only XTQL, SQL or SPARQL, and has never read
    18	Triplex source, can query the stored data and get answers that match what the Triplex entity API
    19	returns at the same basis.
    20	
    21	**Done condition.** The goal is complete when both lanes reach the scope, scale and ambition in the
    22	handoff context (`handoffs/claude-native/README.md`, `wiki/goals.md`):
    23	
    24	1. All ten goals in `wiki/goals.md` pass for `xtdb-native` (XTQL and SQL counted separately) and
    25	   for `qlever-native`, with fresh evidence from this checkout's own engines.
    26	2. Scale target is 10m distinct application entities with 100m representative active domain facts,
    27	   in sparse and representative profiles, with history and write amplification reported separately.
    28	3. Load acceptance uses 256 logical clients with steady writes, then 512 and 1024 client stress under
    29	   a frozen overload policy.
    30	4. The Triplex entity API (the "ORM") and the raw native escape hatch both pass the entity suite on
    31	   both lanes.
    32	
    33	An engine limit is not completion. When an engine blocks a goal, the loop opens the engine-repair
    34	track for that engine; we hold the pinned source of both engines in `references/`.
    35	
    36	## 2. What the research changed
    37	
    38	The first research wave turned the projection from a nice-to-have into a requirement on both lanes.
    39	
    40	| Finding | Source | Consequence |
    41	| --- | --- | --- |
    42	| XTDB 2.2 indexes only `_id`. Other predicates are column scans at about 0.27 s per million rows. | R1 Q7 | The EAV ledger (`_id` = assertion id) turns entity and attribute lookups into full ledger scans. At 100m facts that is tens of seconds per query. Hot reads must resolve through `_id`. |
    43	| QLever's real reified layout is about 30 triples and 724 B per fact; with the journal, `54A + 27E + 63` triples per transaction. | R2, R4 | 3 to 5.7 billion triples at 100m facts, beyond the 60 GiB VM. A compact ledger (about 7 triples) plus a plain current graph (about 1.2) is about 0.8 billion triples and 17 GB. |
    44	| QLever updates cost O(total delta) per request and have no rollback. | R2 Q1 | Writes need one operation per request, a bounded delta with online `rebuild-index`, and roll-forward recovery. |
    45	| XTDB client-set system time is fragile: one abort moves the head to wall-clock. | R1 Q1 | Never set system time. Map commit positions to the system time the engine assigned (hypothesis H-X-CUT below). |
    46	| QLever string order is ICU; a 6-hex-digit-per-codepoint key sorts in codepoint order under the pinned engine, proven exhaustively. | R5 | The string-order blocker has a measured fix: +1 triple and about 58 B per text assertion. |
    47	| QLever EXISTS is planned uncorrelated; re-binding correlated variables inside the group fixes the counterexamples. | R5 | The correlation blocker has a verified authoring fix; planner memory per EXISTS must be costed. |
    48	| XTQL covers 11 of 71 native tasks today, all `state_at`. 57 tasks are SQL only. | R6 | XTQL parity is the largest single body of work in lane X. |
    49	| The XTDB native library has no task-wide fixed cut; XTDB snapshot tokens and fixed-basis read-only transactions work. | R6, R1 Q5 | Multi-call native tasks can pin one cut natively. |
    50	
    51	## 3. Architecture thesis
    52	
    53	### 3.1 T1: ledger plus idiomatic entity projection, written in one atomic step
    54	
    55	The ledger stays the canonical record: exact values, both clocks, assertion identity, retractions,
    56	receipts, journal and claims. Each engine adds a projection in its own idiom, derived from a
    57	content-addressed **projection spec** (EntityTypes and attributes mapped to physical names and
    58	types), and written in the same atomic step as the ledger.
    59	
    60	```
    61	                          Triplex TypeScript API
    62	   +------------------------------------------------------------------+
    63	   | Repository<Model> (typed from EntityType): at(basis) get getMany |
    64	   | find({where, include, limit, cursor}) timeline                   |
    65	   | Writer -> WritePlan -> commit(plans, {actor, commandId})          |
    66	   | native.xtql`` native.sql`` native.sparql`` + declared contract    |
    67	   +--------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    68	                                    |
    69	         current-basis reads, raw   |   exact temporal reads, history,
    70	         queries, index lookups     |   receipts, journal, claims
    71	                     v              |              v
    72	   +--------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    73	   |        ENTITY PROJECTION         |       ASSERTION LEDGER          |
    74	   |                                  |                                 |
    75	   | XTDB                             | XTDB                            |
    76	   |  table per EntityType            |  tx_assertions, tx_meta,        |
    77	   |   _id = entity id                |  tx_claims, tx_attempts         |
    78	   |   ns$attr column per attribute,  |  (existing), plus an entity-    |
    79	   |   one declared type each         |  keyed access path so history   |
    80	   |   native valid time = business   |  reads avoid a ledger scan      |
    81	   |   time                           |                                 |
    82	   |  index tables keyed by value:    | QLever                          |
    83	   |   unique attrs, attr-value,      |  compact assertion nodes        |
    84	   |   reverse refs                   |  (target about 7 triples),      |
    85	   |                                  |  field predicates in their own  |
    86	   | QLever                           |  namespace, order keys for text |
    87	   |  current graph per scope         |  and numbers                    |
    88	   |   <e> rdf:type <Type>            |                                 |
    89	   |   <e> <attr> typed literal       |                                 |
    90	   |   native types only where exact  |                                 |
    91	   +----------------------------------+---------------------------------+
    92	     XTDB: one transaction for ledger, projection and index tables.
    93	     QLever: roll-forward protocol (ledger, verify, watermark, projection).
    94	```
    95	
    96	**Why T1:**
    97	
    98	| Option | Raw queries make sense | Exact semantics | Meets 100m-fact scale | Role |
    99	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   100	| A0: ledger only (status quo) | no: EAV pivots, reified joins | yes | no (scans in XTDB; 3 to 5.7B triples in QLever) | control baseline |
   101	| A1: projection only | yes | no: loses assertion identity and exact recorded cuts | maybe | rejected after research |
   102	| T1: ledger plus projection | yes | yes | target | primary |
