lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:100:- Native `_system_from/_system_to` on projection rows then mean "engine time of the projection write". They will not equal Triplex `recordedAt` unless option 2 is accepted.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:104:## Q2. Valid-time DML: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, ERASE, PATCH
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:112:| `INSERT INTO t (_id, _valid_from, _valid_to, ...)` | Both valid-time columns optional. Default is now (the tx system time) to end of time. Replaces the whole document for that period | `sql/txs.md:28`, probes p2 steps 1-2 |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:114:| Two `INSERT`s for one `_id` in one tx | Second wins, and only one version exists (same system time) | probe p2 step 15 |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:121:| `ERASE FROM t WHERE ...` | Removes every version in all valid time and all system time. `FOR ALL SYSTEM_TIME FOR ALL VALID_TIME` then returns zero rows | `sql/txs.md:86-97`, probe p2 step 17 |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:145:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:155:## Q3. Columns, names and types
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:157:**Verdict.** An attribute key `:person/name` becomes column `person$name`, reachable from XTQL as `person/name` and from SQL as `person$name`. The normalisation is lossy (case-folding, `-` to `_`). Wire types are well behaved for monomorphic columns and surprising for unions, sets, structs and `URI`. Tables get sparse columns on first write with no DDL.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:175:| XTQL `[xt/valid-from xt/system-from]` | Resolve to `_valid_from`, `_system_from` |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:206:| set | 114 (JSON) | array | `PgType.kt:989-990`. Set identity is lost on read |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:210:- **Unions.** A column's type is the join of every value ever written and only widens (`docs/.../about/types-in-xtdb.md`). A union (for example `#{:i64 :utf8}` or `#{:f64 :i64}`) is sent as OID 114 JSON. In the probe, `c_i64 = 9007199254740993` read back as `9007199254740992` after another row put text in the same column, because the value travelled as a JSON number. A double column that received one integer literal became `#{:f64 :i64}`. The repo client's rule (safe integer is OID 20, else OID 701) therefore creates unions whenever a float attribute holds a whole number. A projection must send floats with an explicit float type every time.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:213:- **Heterogeneous rows and later columns.** Tables are sparse and schemaless. A column appears on first write, `NULL` and "missing" are the same value, and `what-is-xtdb.md:38` says tables are "always 'sparse' ... and 'wide'". Upstream #5714 (compaction failure "cannot promote DUV leg" after column presence and type changes across generations) is closed with fix commits dated to 2026-09-22, which is before the 2.2.0 tag. Inclusion in the 2.2.0 image was not verified.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:214:- **Column count.** No limit was hit. A single document with 8,001 columns inserted in 228 ms and `SELECT *` returned all 8,001 columns in 217 ms (probe p9). Upstream PR #6200 (draft, not in 2.2.0) reports that building the metadata page index costs time proportional to columns times pages, so very wide tables get slower to open.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:219:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:222:- Because normalisation lowercases and folds `-` to `_`, two Triplex attributes that differ only in case or `-` versus `_` would share one column. The projection needs a collision check at schema deploy time.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:229:## Q4. XTQL surface in 2.2.0
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:231:**Verdict.** The documented XTQL operators all work through `XTQL ('...', $n)`, including joins, aggregates, subqueries and parameters. `union-all` parses but cannot be planned. SQL window functions beyond `ROW_NUMBER`, `LEAD`, `LAG` fail with `AbstractMethodError`, which reproduces the repo's finding. No release after 2.2.0 exists, so no fix is available.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:239:| `from` (vector bind, map with `:bind`, `:for-valid-time`, `:for-system-time`) | Works | `xtql.clj:357-399`. `{:email "x"}` binds a constant. `*` projects all columns |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:250:| `union-all` | **Parses, fails at planning**: "No implementation of method: :plan-query of protocol ... xtdb.xtql.UnionAll" | `xtql.clj:611-616`. Not in the docs |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:267:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:269:- Raw user XTQL such as `(from :person [name email])` works with the projection, subject to the naming rules in Q3.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:270:- A user who writes `SUM(x) OVER (...)` against projection tables gets the same `AbstractMethodError`. The projection cannot fix it, so docs for native queries should say so.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:275:## Q5. Temporal reads, snapshots and basis tokens
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:277:**Verdict.** The read surface is complete and behaves as documented: valid-time and system-time filters in SQL and XTQL, per-query and per-transaction basis, snapshot tokens for repeatable cuts, await tokens for read-your-writes.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:281:*Syntax.* `FOR VALID_TIME AS OF t | FROM a TO b | BETWEEN a AND b | ALL`, the same for `SYSTEM_TIME`, `FOR ALL VALID_TIME`, and `FOR VALID_TIME ONLY FROM a TO b` (v2.2+, clamps returned bounds to the window, `queries.md:186-205`). XTQL: `:for-valid-time` / `:for-system-time` with `(at t)`, `(in a b)`, `(from t)`, `(to t)` or `:all-time` (`xtql.clj:233-262`). The `#inst` reader literal inside XTQL text has millisecond precision. Use a typed `timestamptz` parameter or SQL for microseconds.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:294:| `FOR ALL SYSTEM_TIME FOR ALL VALID_TIME` | 6 rows with exact `_system_from/_system_to` | full history |
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:309:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:317:## Q6. Transactions, preconditions and failure reporting
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:319:**Verdict.** A transaction is atomic across any number of tables. `ASSERT` provides compare-and-set. A failed `ASSERT` surfaces at `COMMIT SYNC` as SQLSTATE `P0004` with the supplied message and is recorded in `xt.txs`. `METADATA` gives a queryable idempotency key.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:325:- **Recording.** `xt.txs` holds one row per transaction with columns `_id, committed, error, system_time, user_metadata`. A failed assertion appears as `committed = false` with error text `[:conflict :xtdb/assert-failed "triplex-cas-conflict" {:tx-op-idx 0, :sql "ASSERT ..."}]`. A parameterised assertion adds `:arg-idx`.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:326:- **Other failures.** Invalid valid times, planning errors, a type error (`v + 'x'`) and `1 / 0` fail at the statement, SQLSTATE `08P01`, before `COMMIT`, and `xt.txs` also recorded an aborted row for the type error and the division by zero. After any error the connection needs `ROLLBACK` (the repo client does this). `invalid-system-time` fails at `COMMIT SYNC` with `08P01` (Q1).
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:333:  4. `COMMIT ASYNC` returns before indexing. `SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN` then a read on the same connection waits for it (probe).
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:347:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:349:- The projection write and the ledger write belong in one `BEGIN READ WRITE ... COMMIT SYNC`, guarded by the existing CAS `ASSERT` on `tx_meta`. No new mechanism is needed.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:350:- Any projection-level uniqueness check on a non-`_id` column would scan the table inside the writer. Give such columns their own table keyed by the value (`_id = email`), so the check becomes an `_id` lookup.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:355:## Q7. Performance at scale
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:357:**Verdict.** Ingest is fast with small parameterised batches. `_id` lookups stay flat as the table grows. Every non-`_id` predicate is a column scan whose cost grows linearly with table size. There are no secondary indexes in 2.2.0. A 10-million-row projection table will answer non-`_id` filters in seconds, not milliseconds.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:364:                                  v live index (memory) --every 102,400 rows or 15 min--> L0 block file (Arrow)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:366:   compactor: L0 -> L1 (coalesce toward ~100 MB) -> L2 (4-way split by IID bit prefix) -> L3 ...
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:367:   per-page (about 1,024 rows) metadata: numeric/date min/max, bloom on string/binary (compacted levels only)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:370:- Events (put, delete, erase) are sorted by IID (16-byte hash of `_id`) then system time descending, in Arrow files (`dev/doc/high-level-tour.adoc:108-112`, `dev/GLOSSARY.md`, "LSM tree" and "IID").
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:372:- Page metadata: numeric, date-time, time and duration min/max; bloom filters on UTF-8 and binary columns. `MetadataFlavour.kt:77` marks DECIMAL as unsupported for metadata. A table with DECIMAL columns still flushed to an L0 block in the probe, and compaction of DECIMAL columns was not tested (open question 10). L0 blocks are written without blooms except for `_iid` (`LiveTable.kt:198`, `ColumnMetadata.kt:141`). Only compaction output computes blooms for all string columns (`Compactor.kt:91`).
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:373:- **No secondary indexes.** `docs/.../concepts/key-concepts.md:109-114` ("no concept of uniqueness beyond the ID"). Upstream #3663 ("Maintaining user-specified secondary indices") is open, labelled `spec-reqd`, blocked by #5733, and describes a future `CREATE INDEX` with hash-keyed LSM indexes for equality only. It states that today "a predicate on a non-key column typically leads to scanning" and that page metadata is "under-selective".
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:413:After a node restart (cold caches) at 3M rows the first run was slower (point lookup 419 ms, unique email 1,997 ms, array scan 10.1 s) and later runs matched the warm numbers. Extrapolating the linear scans to 10M rows gives about 2.8 s for a non-`_id` equality, 2.3 s for `COUNT(*)` and 25 s for an array-membership filter on this 2-CPU VM. The unique-email lookup cost about as much as a single-column scan even after compaction produced L1 and L2 files with bloom metadata, so bloom pruning was not visible on sequentially inserted emails. That conclusion rests on one data shape.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:425:Live steady-state size is about 200 B per row of data plus metadata for this 10-column shape. The `buffers` directory was about 5.5 times larger than live data because compaction writes cumulative intermediate L1 files (sizes 7 MB, 23 MB, 39 MB, ... 118 MB for one growing file) that stay until garbage collection (`garbageLifetime: PT24H`, `blocksToKeep: 10`, `ops/config.md:297-300`). Plan disk for that transient.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:433:*Memory knobs.* JVM flags through `JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS` (`-Xmx`, `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize`). The image sets none, so heap defaults to 25% of the container limit and direct memory to the heap size. `memoryCache.maxSizeBytes` or `maxSizeRatio` (default 0.5 of max direct memory), `diskCache` only for remote storage, `indexer.rowsPerBlock`, `indexer.flushDuration`, `compactor.threads` (default `min(availableProcessors / 2, 1)`, effectively 1), `garbageCollector.*` (`ops/config.md:84-101,239-300`). The repo's `compose.xtdb.yaml` sets `-Xmx3g -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=2g` on an 8 GB container with `memoryCache.maxSizeBytes: 536870912`. Upstream #4756 ("Document minimum memory config") is open.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:437:**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:439:- Raw XTQL on a projection table with a predicate on any column except `_id` costs a column scan. At 10M rows that is seconds. Hot lookups (by email, by external key) need a derived table keyed by the lookup value.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:440:- Plan on about 200 bytes per row per version for a 10-column entity, multiplied by the number of valid-time slices and system-time versions. A 30-attribute entity pays for the whole row on every attribute change.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:445:## Top 5 design-relevant facts
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:448:2. **Native system time is a single strictly increasing microsecond clock shared by every transaction, including aborts and DDL.** Client-set values are possible, but one CAS-conflict abort moves the head to wall-clock now and breaks any logical clock that is behind it. Treat engine time as engine-assigned and store a position-to-time map via `METADATA`. (Q1)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:449:3. **Only `_id` is indexed.** `_id` lookups stay near 5 ms to 3M rows. Every other predicate is a column scan at about 0.27 s per million rows, and an `ASSERT` on a non-`_id` column took 1.8 s at 3M rows inside the single writer. (Q6, Q7)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:450:4. **Names and wire types need deliberate handling.** `:ns/attr` is physically `ns$attr`; XTQL lowercases and folds `-` to `_`; SQL cannot write `/`; unions go to the client as JSON (integer precision loss in JS); `URI` cannot be selected; sets need transit; json-typed parameters are stored as text; a missing SQL column is an error unless pre-declared with `CREATE OR ALTER TABLE`. (Q3)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:451:5. **The write path should be small same-text parameterised batches in one `BEGIN READ WRITE`, guarded by the existing CAS `ASSERT` (SQLSTATE `P0004`).** About 10k rows/s, atomic across the ledger and projection tables, with an explicit JVM heap. Large literal `VALUES` statements ran the node out of heap. (Q6, Q7)
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:455:## Open questions needing experiments
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:457:1. **10M-row run.** Load 10M projection rows and measure non-`_id` predicate latency, compaction lag, steady-state disk after garbage collection, and cold-cache behaviour. Everything above is extrapolated from 1M and 3M.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:458:2. **Bloom pruning on shuffled data.** Repeat the unique-string lookup with randomly ordered inserts, after full compaction, and read `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` pruning output to see whether any pages are skipped.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:459:3. **Update amplification for wide entities.** For a 30 to 100 column entity, apply N single-attribute `PATCH`es across M valid slices and measure rows, bytes and compaction cost per version.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:462:6. **Writer contention.** Measure commit throughput and tail latency with many concurrent connections doing CAS `ASSERT` on one `tx_meta` row, including how many aborts occur and how each abort moves the system-time head.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:463:7. **Snapshot token lifetime.** Check that a `SNAPSHOT_TOKEN` still resolves the same cut after a node restart, after compaction, and on a second node.
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:466:10. **Decimal and union columns through compaction.** Insert DECIMAL and mixed-type columns across several blocks, force compaction, and re-read (relevant to #5714 and to `MetadataFlavour.kt:77`).
lab/research/R1-xtdb-capabilities.md:467:11. **XTQL `union-all`.** Confirm whether any planner path supports it or whether it must be removed from user-facing guidance.
