lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:214:## 3. Precise explanations
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:216:### 3.1 The five failing "historical retirement-metadata" checks (configured-entity tiny v4)
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:227:1. `sparse-old-cut-after-all-writes:retirement-metadata-target`
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:228:2. `dense-old:retirement-metadata-target`
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:229:3. `corrected:retirement-metadata-target`
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:230:4. `absent:retirement-metadata-target`
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:231:5. `dense-recorded-R-with-returned-source-P:retirement-metadata-target`
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:237:  Eleven such future-retirement rows appear across ten typed snapshots (100 lifetime versions retained separately).
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:238:- The new target says an as-of snapshot must show those two fields as `null` until the retirement was known at the cut.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:239:  Counterexample file in the review: assertion `00A35M0J000000000000000001`, R = 1001.25, returned P = 7, retired at 1005.25 by `_tx/01B681NY000000000000000001`; target expects `retractedAt: null, retractTxId: null`.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:247:- The native source relation already does the masking. SQL: `CASE WHEN <knownRetraction> THEN retracted_at ELSE NULL END` for all three retirement columns
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:256:  Only the finite-nine event oracle has independent acceptance. Presence and type-version are explicit application facts; codec `schemaVersion = Some(1)` is a different thing.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:260:### 3.2 Writer liveness and the 20 identity lookups per writer
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:265:- Each writer issues **22 SELECTs before `BEGIN`**: 20 identity lookups, 1 basis read, 1 command-claim read. With a `SET AWAIT_TOKEN` before each, that is 44 driver statements.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:266:- Over six writers: 120 identity statements form 60 pairs with equal SQL hash and equal typed parameters, each returning exactly one row.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:268:- About 94% of aggregate ACK wall time (0.939) is before `BEGIN`; 91% (0.913) is inside the identity lookups' driver spans
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:269:  (`qualification/load-waves/xtdb-identity-read-hypotheses-v1/plan.json`).
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:270:  These are driver wall spans. They include network, server and observer time; no engine CPU or index timestamp exists.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:301:safe and unsafe CAS rejections (`P0004`) and rollbacks match; all 15 pools and 301/285 leases closed. Not integrated, not run on full142, no performance claim, and the source is not in this checkout.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:308:### 3.3 The XTDB 2.2 window-function `AbstractMethodError` and the workaround
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:310:**What happened.** Native packet v1 ran all 142 cases and workload 18 (machine uptime interval union) hit an `AbstractMethodError` for an aggregate window query
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:311:(`wiki/research.md` "Cross-lane pinned-version finding"; comment at `xtdb-workloads.ts:527-531`). A second bug in the same area was avoided: a "LongVector union/join" issue
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:333:- `ROW_NUMBER` and `LAG/LEAD` do work in this engine, but a union of intervals needs a running maximum, which needs an aggregate window. An island-start formulation (`NOT EXISTS` an earlier interval that reaches this start)
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:338:### 3.4 Recorded time versus XTDB system time
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:343: recordedAt  R   (binary64)       column tx_assertions.recorded_at      _system_from / _system_to
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:344: recordedPosition P (integer)     column recorded_position, tx_meta     tx id / system time
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:346: retractedAt / retractedPosition  columns retracted_at / _position      system-time version of the row
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:351:1. **Different clocks.** Triplex R comes from the Triplex runtime clock (tests use `deterministic.startTime`, e.g. 1000.25; `native-entry.ts:131-137`). XTDB system time is assigned at
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:353:   (`references/xtdb/core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/indexer/TxResolver.kt:209-235 @ c6c1962`). A caller can propose a system time but it must be later than the latest
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:354:   completed transaction (`TxResolver.kt:219-232`). R and system time can never be compared by value.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:357:3. **Retraction rewrites the row.** A retract re-INSERTs the whole row at the same `_id` with `_valid_from` 1970, so XTDB keeps the earlier version at an earlier system time. That makes
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:358:   `FOR ALL SYSTEM_TIME` a possible source for "the row as it was before retraction" (the stronger snapshot target), but nothing maps R or P to a system time.
lab/research/R6-xtdb-gap-analysis.md:372:## 4. The ten goals for xtdb-native
