lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:92:### 2.2 Why raw queries do not yet read like XTQL, SQL or SPARQL
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:100:## 3. Prior art, source by source
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:102:### 3.1 LDkit (schema to SPARQL)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:146:### 3.2 Drizzle ORM (typed tables, `sql` tag, relational queries)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:180:### 3.3 Datascript and Datomic (entity API, pull, database values)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:182:[Datascript](https://github.com/tonsky/datascript) at `34915cf`, and [Datomic](https://docs.datomic.com) documentation.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:187:- The entity API reads lazily. A cardinality-many attribute returns a set. A cardinality-one ref returns another entity. `:_attr` navigates backwards [clone src/datascript/impl/entity.cljc:20-27, 175-187]. Datomic's page says the same, and adds that entities are not meant as a mapping layer and are unsuitable for existence tests [web https://docs.datomic.com/reference/entities.html].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:188:- `pull` takes a pattern. Map specs expand refs, `_` reverses, `:limit` bounds many-valued results (default 1000), `:default` fills gaps, and missing attributes are omitted from the result. Recursion returns only the id when it meets an entity it already saw [web https://docs.datomic.com/query/query-pull.html].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:189:- Time is a database value. `d/as-of`, `d/since`, `d/history` and `d/filter` each take a db value and return a db value that every query and pull accepts unchanged [web https://docs.datomic.com/reference/filters.html]. Datascript has `filter` [clone src/datascript/core.cljc:247-263] and no history [clone README.md:278].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:191:**Excerpt** [web https://docs.datomic.com/reference/filters.html; clone src/datascript/impl/entity.cljc:20-27].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:209:- Triplex already has a batched `entities` call, which is what Datomic's peer cache gives the entity API for free.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:213:- Datomic has one time axis. Business time is an ordinary attribute there. Triplex needs `validFrom` and `validTo` on the assertion.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:219:### 3.4 XTDB 2 data modeling
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:225:- Tables are schemaless. Every row needs `_id`. Four temporal columns exist on every table: `_system_from`, `_system_to`, `_valid_from`, `_valid_to`. A row is one version of a record [clone references/xtdb/docs/src/content/docs/concepts/key-concepts.md:25-70].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:254:- A wide row version covers all columns for one valid-time interval, while Triplex intervals differ per attribute. Fragmenting is possible but loses assertion identity.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:256:- XTDB system time is assigned by XTDB. Triplex recorded time and commit position are assigned by the Triplex boundary. The handoff forbids equating them without proof.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:261:### 3.5 Bitemporal and provenance data on RDF
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:263:The question is how to attach valid time, recorded time and assertion identity to a fact and still leave a plain triple that ordinary SPARQL can match.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:267:| Option | Plain triple exists? | Assertion identity | Time on the fact | Works on QLever 0.6 | Leak risk for a naive query |
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:271:| C. Named graph per assertion: the plain triple inside `GRAPH <assertion-iri>`, metadata about the IRI in another graph | Yes, inside its own graph | The graph IRI | Metadata triples about the graph IRI | Yes (QLever supports quads and `GRAPH ?g`) `[clone references/qlever/README.md:17]` | A bare `{ ?s p ?o }` without `GRAPH` reads the union of all graphs |
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:279:- Hernandez, Hogan and Kroetzsch compared standard reification, n-ary relations, singleton properties and named graphs on five engines. Singleton properties were the most compact representation, but four of the five engines struggled with the large number of distinct predicates they create, and one engine struggled with a large number of named graphs. There was no clear winner among standard reification, n-ary and named graphs [web https://iccl.inf.tu-dresden.de/w/images/5/52/Hernandez-Hogan-Kroetzsch-RDF-reification-evaluation-SSWS2015.pdf, Section 6, text extracted with `pdftotext`].
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:281:- Fernandez, Umbrich, Polleres and Knuth classify archive storage as independent copies, change-based, timestamp-based and hybrid [web https://www.semantic-web-journal.net/node/1814]. OSTRICH stores snapshots plus delta chains with add and delete indexes and answers triple patterns at, between and across versions, but it is not a SPARQL engine [web https://ruben.verborgh.org/publications/taelman_jws_2019/]. Triplex's model is the timestamp-based family, so no engine can answer "what is true at T" from a bare pattern. Either a filter or a materialized snapshot is always required.
