lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:421:### 4.3 RDF (QLever)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:423:#### 4.3.1 IRI scheme
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:437:#### 4.3.2 Typed literals
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:452:#### 4.3.3 Entity IRI encoding
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:475:#### 4.3.4 The plain triple, the class, and the assertion node
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:480:# scope graph <urn:triplex:scope:dGVzdA>   (existing field triples, plus one new)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:489:# assertion graph <urn:triplex:assertion:QUJD>   (NEW, two triples)
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:496:- **Assertion identity** is the graph IRI. It is also the subject of the engine fields, so a join on `?a` links the plain triple to its validity, recording and retraction.
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:498:- **Added volume** is three triples and one graph per published assertion, against roughly two dozen triples per assertion today (counted from `rowFields`, not measured on an index). The one-graph-per-assertion cost at 100 million assertions is unmeasured (P3).
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:517:#### 4.3.5 What is deliberately not done
lab/research/R3-orm-prior-art.md:520:- **No `FROM <scope>` injection.** With `FROM` and no `FROM NAMED` the dataset's named-graph set is empty under SPARQL 1.1 section 13.2 (QLever's `DatasetClauses.h:44-48` mirrors the default-graph half of that rule; the named-graph half is part of P4), so the assertion graphs would vanish. Isolation comes from the join to the scope graph inside `visible()`. A bare `GRAPH ?a { ... }` still reads other tenants' plain triples, so physical tenant isolation (one QLever index per tenant) or a host-level boundary stays necessary. Authorization is host-owned in Triplex already `[repo docs/agents.md, anti-pattern table]`.
