lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:1:# R5. QLever 0.6.0 semantic blockers, root causes and ranked fixes
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:3:Date 2026-11-07. Pinned engine is QLever `v0.6.0`, commit `6c5861a6526d7d7ae77735d09339ee0825f0c001` (cited below as `@6c5861a`). Source read from `references/qlever` (clean checkout, `git describe` = `v0.6.0`). Probes ran against `adfreiburg/qlever:0.6.0@sha256:37d5ede1…49a78ec`, whose index metadata records `git-hash 6c5861a` and `has-icu-support true`.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:5:## 1. Answer first
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:9:| **String order** (`ORDER BY STR(...)`, ranges, pages) | The pinned engine has exactly one string comparator, ICU collation at IDENTICAL strength for locale `en_US`. Stored words, `STR()` results and constants all go through it. No runtime setting switches it to codepoint order. | **Schema-derived maintained order key.** One extra lowercase hex string per orderable text assertion, 6 hex digits per Unicode codepoint, compared with `<`, `>`, `ORDER BY` on the key instead of on the text. | Proved under the pinned image on 4,368 exhaustive strings, 47,988 base-36 strings, 400 random Unicode keys, 136 range checks, a 150-key post-index `INSERT DATA` delta, and pagination. Not yet wired into the SDK. |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:14:## 2. Evidence that existed and evidence that is missing
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:17:- **Missing from this checkout.** The wiki cites `qlever-native-str-order-semantics-v1.json`, `qlever-native-str-order-source-locators-v1.json`, `qlever-wrapper-string-range-numeric-guard-v1.json`, `qlever-native-shape-edges-independent-v1.json`, `qlever-public-string-extension-v6-author-terminal.json` and the `.devloop` terminals (`qlever-native-page-sample-v1/0001`). None of them are present. I recovered the exact queries from the source that produced them (`native-query-shapes.ts:141-146`, the tiny-control fixture and oracle) and the v6 numbers from the independent terminal review. I did not invent any missing hash or result.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:20:## 3. Blocker A, string order
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:22:### 3.1 Counterexamples, exact
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:24:**A1. Tiny native control (9 triples).** Source `qualification/native-controls/qlever-collation-tiny-v1/{fixture.nt,oracle.json}`, status `SOURCE_ONLY_UNEXECUTED` until this report.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:26:| Entity suffix | Label codepoints | Codepoint rank (oracle) | ICU rank (observed) |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:41:- **Ranges (T1c to T1e).** `STR(?l) > "E"` returned `ascii-z, astral-10000, nfc-e-acute, nfd-e-acute, private-use` and omitted `ascii-c` and `emoji`. `STR(?l) < "c"` returned `ascii-A, emoji` and omitted `ascii-E`. The oracle requires `> "E"` = `c, nfd, z, nfc, private-use, astral, emoji` and `< "c"` = `A, E`.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:42:- **Oracle source.** `oracle.json`, independently authored before execution. It cites SPARQL 1.1 `STR`, `ORDER BY` and operator mapping (simple literals compare by Unicode codepoint). The oracle contains no engine output and no casefold.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:48:- **Expected.** ASCII ascending `A, E, c, z`. Original-cut continuation after the correction `c, z`. Unicode ascending `unicode:00..06` in codepoint order. `STR(?label) > U+E000` returns `unicode:05, unicode:06`. Source `qualification/extensions/string-order-v1/oracle.json` (SHA `8f4da85e…`) and `policy.json`. The ASCII policy `A<E<c<z` is the root-approved narrow policy. The native Unicode cases are normative SPARQL codepoint semantics.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:55:### 3.2 Root cause, with citations
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:84:Stored words: the vocabulary is sorted with the same comparator at index build
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:91:- **ICU is selected at build time, not at run time.** `LocaleManager` is a type alias for `LocaleManagerICU` or `LocaleManagerNoICU` (`LocaleManager.h:429-434`), chosen by the CMake option `QLEVER_NO_UNICODE` (`CMakeLists.txt:325-332`). The index records `has-icu-support` and refuses a mismatched binary (`IndexImpl.cpp:1325, 1430-1442`). The only run-time knob is `locale {language, country, ignore-punctuation}` (`IndexImpl.cpp:1618-1648`).
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:94:### 3.3 Probe output (container `tx2-probe-r5`, image above, removed afterwards)
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:99:T1a ORDER BY STR(?l) actual : emoji, ascii-A, ascii-c, ascii-E, nfd-e-acute, nfc-e-acute, ascii-z, astral-10000, private-use   -> FAIL vs oracle
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:107:T3b/T3c 150 keys added by INSERT DATA after index build (local vocabulary) ORDER BY == codepoint order  True
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:115:## 4. Blocker B, correlated `NOT EXISTS`
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:117:### 4.1 Counterexamples, exact
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:140:### 4.2 Root cause, with citations
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:169:### 4.3 Probe matrix (`probe1.out`, `probe3.out`)
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:194:## 5. Ranked fix hypotheses
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:198:### 5.1 String order
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:202:| 1 | **S1. Schema-derived maintained order key** | Writer emits a second literal per orderable text assertion: lowercase hex, 6 digits per codepoint, in a new channel. Reads order, range and page on the key. | Fixes every authored Triplex ordering, range and page on keyed attributes, ASCII and full Unicode, with the pinned image. Does not fix ad-hoc `ORDER BY STR(?x)` on unkeyed values. | Write: +1 triple per text assertion, computed in TypeScript in the same `INSERT DATA`. Storage: measured +57.7 B per assertion (24-codepoint text, 200k assertions, +11.5 MB), split 35 B vocabulary and 22 B permutation entries. Rebuild: none for new data. Backfill: one streamed pass over existing string assertions. Freshness: none, the assertion is immutable, retirement adds `retracted_*` triples and never touches the key. | Medium. Every consumer must use the key (Datalog `scalar()`, native `ctx.fact`), or ordering silently falls back to ICU. Encoder changes need a new predicate name. | Section 5.3 tests, then the v6 extension against a candidate. |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:203:| 2 | **S2. Byte-collation build (`QLEVER_NO_UNICODE=ON`) plus full reindex** | Upstream compile flag in 0.6.0. `LocaleManagerNoICU::compare` is `a.compare(b)` on the inner text (`LocaleManager.h:384-389`), which is unsigned byte order, equal to codepoint order for valid UTF-8. | Likely makes every `STR`, `ORDER BY` and range filter codepoint-correct, including hand-written SPARQL. The only candidate besides S3 that does. Not yet shown, because no NoICU binary exists. | Reindex of all data, new image, `has-icu-support=false` index incompatible with the current one (`IndexImpl.cpp:1430-1442`). Storage: none extra. Freshness: none. | High. Upstream warns Unicode features degrade (`CMakeLists.txt:325`). `LCASE`, `UCASE`, normalization and text-index tokenization become ASCII-only. Corner case still to test, strings containing `"` or `\` (the comparator parses the stored literal with `findLiteralEnd`). | The existing tiny-control proposal, run on a NoICU image (build in 5.5). |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:204:| 3 | **S3. Patch a runtime or build-time bytewise vocabulary comparator that keeps ICU functions** | Add a collation mode that reuses NoICU `compare`, `getSortKey`, `getPrefixSortKey` while `getLowercaseUtf8` and `normalizeUtf8` keep ICU. Estimated 150 to 300 lines including the mismatch guard and tests. | Same order effect as S2 without losing Unicode functions. | Same reindex as S2. Carries a private fork. | High. Touches vocabulary, local vocabulary, prefix ranges, text index, and the index-compatibility guard. Upstream is moving the other way (#2910 drops sort-key logic). | Same tiny control, then the full v6 extension. |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:205:| 4 | **S4. Index locale `en`/`US_POSIX`** | Settings JSON only (`"country":"US_POSIX"`). ICU's POSIX tailoring orders ASCII by codepoint. | Fixes ASCII mixed case and ASCII ranges. Does **not** fix the Unicode cases. Probe: ASCII subset exact, tiny fixture still fails, 399 of 400 random positions differ (emoji sorts first, U+10000 and U+E000 last). | Full reindex. No code. | Medium. QLever logs that non-default locales are "untested". Fails the normative native Unicode oracle. Useful only if the product policy is ASCII-only. | Already run (probe2). |
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:210:### 5.2 Correlated `NOT EXISTS`
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:221:### 5.3 Why hex order keys sort in codepoint order under QLever's comparator
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:231:5. **Option defaults.** Numeric collation and alternate-shifted handling are off for `en_US` with `ignore-punctuation=false` (`IndexImpl.cpp:1618-1648`). The recorded locale in the probe index is `{language:"en", country:"US", ignore-punctuation:false}`.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:248:- Storage was measured on a synthetic 200k-assertion corpus with two triples each. The Triplex layout writes about 30 to 35 triples per assertion (my count of `rowFields`, not a measured index), so the relative cost against a full Triplex index is smaller than in the probe. Measure it on the real layout before quoting a percentage.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:252:### 5.4 Exact equivalence conditions for the authoring rewrites
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:262:2. `B` is a copy of the conjunct of `O` that bound `x`, built from the same fact helper so it carries the same graph, cut, valid-time and retirement filters. μ therefore satisfies `B`.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:275:### 5.5 Patching and building QLever, feasibility
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:280:- **Estimate for our VM** (unmeasured). The Colima `triplex2` VM has 6 CPUs and 12 GiB, shared with other probe containers. Building only `qlever-index` and `qlever-server` with `RUN_TESTS=false` under `--cpus 3 --memory 6g` and `ninja -j3` plausibly takes 45 to 120 minutes and a few GiB of RAM per compile job. The Dockerfile comment says arm64 CI builds crashed from the OOM killer before mitigations (`mold`, deferred test discovery). `CHEAPER_COMPILATION=ON` exists but trades runtime speed, so it should not be used for a binary whose performance is measured.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:284:## 6. Cross-links that matter for the planner-memory problem
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:286:- QLever issue #3190 (merged 2026-10-24, after 0.6.0) reports that planning time and memory grew roughly fourfold with each `FILTER EXISTS` or `FILTER NOT EXISTS` in a group, and that a four-clause query on an eight-triple index needed 5 GB, which is the same signature as the family12 OOM at 2 GiB. Issue #3573 (open) reports factor-10 growth per nesting level. Issue #2667 (open) reports memory and timeouts from many `FILTER EXISTS`. This is a hypothesis for `qlever-planner-memory.md` and is untested here. It suggests C1/C2 add an `EXISTS` per correlated check, so every re-bind should be counted against the 2 GiB planner budget.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:288:## 7. Recommended first experiment per blocker
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:294:3. Re-run the v6 string extension (`qualification/extensions/string-order-v1`) with native queries that order, range and page on the key. Pass criteria are the frozen oracle arrays unchanged: ASCII `A,E,c,z`, continuation `c,z`, Unicode codepoint order, `> U+E000` range.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:304:## 8. Limits of this report
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:306:- **Probe scope.** One 5,205-triple index plus one 200k-triple storage sample. Hex-key order was verified for strings of 1 to 3 characters exhaustively and up to 30 characters randomly, not for arbitrary lengths. The ICU argument in 5.3 covers arbitrary length, and the probe gate in E-S1 guards a future image change.
lab/research/R5-qlever-semantic-blockers.md:311:## 9. Files
