lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:1:# R2: What QLever 0.6.0 supports and what it costs, for the entity-projection design
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:5:## How to read this report
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:11:- **Probe environment.** One container `tx2-probe-r2` (since removed), `--cpus 2 --memory 3g`, image above, `DOCKER_HOST=unix:///Users/sam/.colima/triplex2/docker.sock`. Server flags matched the repo's (`-j 16 -c 128M -e 32M --persist-updates --no-patterns --no-access-check`), except `-m` was 1G (and 300M/2G for memory tests) and the server ran with its working directory inside the index directory (needed for `rebuild-index`, see 1.7).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:12:- **Probe data** came from the repo's real writer code (`rowFields` in `packages/qlever/src/rdf.ts`, via `gen.ts`): 400,000 facts over 80,000 entities, written in three layouts: **R** (current reified assertion layout), **C** (compact assertion, 7 triples per fact), **P** (plain entity projection). Generator, scripts, transcripts, build logs: `/Users/sam/Claude/Projects/triplex-db-2/R2-qlever-probe/` (`gen.ts`, `gen-out/`, `probe-scripts/`, `build-logs/`).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:17:## 0. Verdicts at a glance
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:21:| 1 | Updates | Full SPARQL 1.1 Update works, default graph is the union of all graphs, an online `rebuild-index` exists, but every update request costs O(total delta), delta memory is not covered by `-m`, and a failed or timed-out update is not rolled back (measured partial leak, measured permutation inconsistency). | High |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:22:| 2 | Collation | ICU only. Default `en_US`, case-insensitive primary order with lowercase first. No runtime binary collation. `en` + country `US_POSIX` gives ASCII byte order for ASCII text only. UPDATE-inserted strings sort identically to index strings. | High |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:31:## 1. Updates
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:33:### 1.1 Verdict
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:36:2. **Default graph.** A query without `FROM` reads the **union of all graphs, deduplicated**. `GRAPH ?g { }` ranges over named graphs only (the built-in default graph is excluded unless `treat-default-graph-as-named-graph=true`). [S] `src/engine/QueryPlanner.cpp:2927-2938 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/CompressedRelation.cpp:292-360 @ 6c5861a`, `src/global/RuntimeParameters.h:182-185 @ 6c5861a`; [P] below.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:39:5. **Compaction.** `cmd=rebuild-index` rebuilds from the live permutations plus delta (no export, no re-sort), then swaps the new index in without a restart. There is also `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:min:max:fraction`. [S][P][W]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:40:6. **Atomicity.** A multi-operation request runs on one update thread and publishes one snapshot at the end. There is **no rollback** on failure or timeout. [S][P] Measured below.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:41:7. **Version token.** None. `cmd=get-index-id` returns a constant (`#.1.1.1.1`), responses carry no ETag. [P]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:43:### 1.2 Evidence: graph semantics (probe P1, default graph plus `G1` and `G2`)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:57:**Graph metadata limit.** Block metadata records at most 20 distinct graphs per block (`src/index/ConstantsIndexBuilding.h:108 @ 6c5861a`). Beyond that a block has no graph info and `FROM` or `GRAPH <g>` filtering is a per-row filter. Two or three graphs stay cheap. One graph per scope (thousands) does not. [S]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:59:### 1.3 Evidence: lifecycle of one update request
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:83:### 1.4 Evidence: memory per delta triple
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:85:- **Structure** [S]: a `LocatedTriple` is a block index (8 B), an `IdTriple<0>` (4 Ids = 32 B) and a flag, 48 B with padding (`src/index/LocatedTriples.h:78-93 @ 6c5861a`). It is stored once per permutation (6), plus a hash set of inserted/deleted triples (`src/index/DeltaTriples.h:160-172 @ 6c5861a`), plus a **second deep copy** for the published snapshot. Terms not in the index vocabulary go to a local vocabulary (heap strings). Lower bound: 288 B per triple for the six copies, roughly doubled during the update.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:94:- **Not covered by `-m`.** With `-m 1G` the server still grew past 3 GiB and died during a second 2.25M-triple insert (total delta 4.5M). The kernel message was not captured and Docker's `OOMKilled` flag was already set by an earlier index-build kill, so the OOM cause is inferred (no assertion text in the log, process gone). [P] Confidence Medium on cause, High on "memory growth is not bounded by `-m`".
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:96:### 1.5 Evidence: `--persist-updates`
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:98:- **What is stored.** After **every** request the server writes the full set of inserted and deleted triples (4 Ids each = 32 B, plus the local vocabulary) to `<base>.update-triples.tmp` and renames it over the old file (`src/index/DeltaTriples.cpp:648-672 @ 6c5861a`). It is a snapshot rewrite, not a log. Internal language-tag triples are regenerated at load. [S]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:104:### 1.6 Evidence: throughput and degradation
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:106:**Update cost grows with the delta** (index P1 = 465k base triples; 25k-triple `INSERT DATA` per request, new subjects and literals):
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:120:**Reads degrade in the blocks the delta touches** (same index, 1.0M delta, cold cache, ms):
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:129:After `rebuild-index` the first row fell to 7.5 ms. The upstream wiki says a 1% change slows affected queries about 2% [W]. My delta was 2x the base index, an extreme case that shows the mechanism rather than a realistic ratio.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:131:### 1.7 Evidence: compaction (`rebuild-index`)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:133:- **It exists and is online.** `curl -d cmd=rebuild-index` (access token unless `--no-access-check`) builds a new index from the current permutations plus delta, then atomically swaps it in. Queries and updates continue during the build. Updates that arrive meanwhile are carried over by a snapshot diff (`DeltaTriples::addFromSnapshotDiff`). [S] `src/engine/Server.cpp:567-580, 1635-1727 @ 6c5861a`, `src/libqlever/Qlever.cpp:754-815 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/IndexRebuilder.cpp:447-632 @ 6c5861a`. v0.6.0 release notes list "swap in the new index at runtime" (#2832) and the automatic strategy (#3163) [W] https://github.com/ad-freiburg/qlever/releases/tag/v0.6.0.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:134:- **No export and re-run of `qlever-index` is needed.** The docs say so and the algorithm is a vocabulary merge plus a streaming permutation rewrite. [W] https://docs.qlever.dev/rebuild-index/
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:135:- **Automatic trigger.** `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:min:max:fraction`, for example `automatic:10000:1000000:0.1`, fires when `inserted + deleted` reaches `clamp(fraction x index_triples, min, max)` (`src/engine/RebuildIndexStrategy.h:25-45 @ 6c5861a`, `src/ServerMain.cpp:168-177 @ 6c5861a`). Default is `manual`. [S]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:136:- **Working-directory trap** [P]. Without `cd` into the index directory the call returns HTTP 400 `The directory "rebuild.<ts>.tmp" is not a subdirectory of the directory of the current index`. The temp and previous directories must be relative and inside the index's own directory (`src/libqlever/Qlever.cpp:484-520 @ 6c5861a`). The repo's start script does `cd /data` with a relative base `triplex`, which satisfies this.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:142:| R: 11.94M base + 100k delta | 12.04M | 4.8 s | 1.33 GB | 3.6M words in 1.4 s, 6 permutations at ~23M triples/s. Scratch build of the same index: 35.2 s |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:144:- **Disk.** New index is built next to the old one. The old index moves to `previous.<build date>/`. Default `--rebuild-keep-previous-index-dirs` is `original-and-most-recent`, so disk use is 2x to 3x the index until pruned. After the swap the persisted delta file shrinks to 55 bytes. [S][P]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:145:- **What is lost.** Text index and loaded materialized views do not carry over (`Server.cpp:1655-1667 @ 6c5861a`). Irrelevant for the repo today. [S]
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:146:- **Published speed.** Under 1 minute for ~500M triples, ~20 minutes for ~10B triples, Ryzen 9 9950X 16 cores, NVMe. [W] https://docs.qlever.dev/rebuild-index/
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:147:- **Tuning knobs** in 0.6.0: `rebuild-index-scan-num-threads` (default 1), `rebuild-permutation-writer-num-threads` (1), `rebuild-max-concurrent-permutation-pairs` (0 = all). The defaults deliberately leave CPU to queries. [S] `src/global/RuntimeParameters.h:65-95 @ 6c5861a`
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:149:### 1.8 Evidence: atomicity of a request, and what failure leaves behind
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:151:**Design** [S]: updates in one request are "atomic" only in the sense that they run back to back on the single update thread (`Server.cpp:1338-1345 @ 6c5861a`), and one snapshot is published after the last one. There is no undo log.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:175:A timed-out update (HTTP 429) was **partially applied, to some permutations only**, and the next successful update published that inconsistent state. The persisted file after restart contained exactly one complete 2.25M-triple set, so the junk was in memory only; a restart discarded it. Source explanation [S]: `locateAndAddTriples` loops over the six permutations and calls `throwIfCancelled()` between them (`DeltaTriples.cpp:200-225 @ 6c5861a`), while the hash sets are marked only afterwards (`DeltaTriples.cpp:476-478 @ 6c5861a`). Confidence High that it happens, Medium on every other consequence (for example I did not test whether a `rebuild-index` after such a state copies the junk into the new permutations).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:181:### 1.9 Implications
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:183:**For the projection design**
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:184:1. Put the entity projection and the assertion history in separate named graphs, and require `FROM <urn:triplex:current>` (or inject it) for user SPARQL. Both graphs share one delta and one rebuild.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:192:- A fact write costs ~1 to 2 delta triples in the plain projection (insert plus delete of the old value), ~7 to 8 in a compact assertion graph, ~30 to 37 in the current reified layout. So 1M delta triples is ~110k facts (compact + plain) or ~33k facts (reified).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:193:- Budget 1M delta triples as the soft ceiling (0.4 to 1.7 GB at rest, up to 2x during an update, ~1.5 s per request, ~94 MB rewritten per request). Turn on `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:<min>:1000000:0.01`-style limits. The right min and fraction depend on the base size and need an experiment (open question 1).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:197:## 2. Vocabulary and collation
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:199:### 2.1 Verdict
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:201:1. **Locale settings (build time only).** `settings.json` key `locale` with `language`, `country`, `ignore-punctuation`. Default `en` / `US` / `false`. Changing it requires a full rebuild (the builder warns so). The locale is stored in the index metadata and the server reads it from there. [S] `src/index/IndexImpl.cpp:1608-1649 @ 6c5861a`, `src/global/Constants.h:266-268 @ 6c5861a`; [P] `idx.meta-data.json` shows the locale.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:202:2. **Collation level.** The vocabulary order and the local-vocabulary comparator both use level `TOTAL`: ICU IDENTICAL, then a byte compare of the full literal text, then the language tag or datatype suffix. The first character of the stored term (`"` for literals, `<` for IRIs) is compared before the collation key, so all literals sort before all IRIs. [S] `src/index/vocabulary/StringSortComparator.h:308-335 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/LocalVocabContextImpl.cpp:15-19 @ 6c5861a`.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:203:3. **No binary collation at run time.** The only true byte order is the build-time CMake option `QLEVER_NO_UNICODE` (ICU-free build, "plain ASCII bytes", index metadata records `has-icu-support` and a mismatched binary refuses the index). It is not in the Docker image. [S] `CMakeLists.txt:325-327 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/IndexImpl.cpp:1430-1442 @ 6c5861a`.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:205:5. **ORDER BY.** Rows compare by `ValueId`. String terms compare by vocabulary index (which is the ICU order) and local-vocabulary terms compare through the same comparator via their cached position in the vocabulary. Values of incompatible datatypes order by datatype: boolean, then numeric (compared numerically), then strings (plain and language-tagged together), then date-times. [S] `src/engine/OrderBy.cpp:105-115 @ 6c5861a`, `src/global/ValueIdComparators.h:480-560 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/LocalVocabEntry.cpp:13-45 @ 6c5861a`; [P] P3.5, P15.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:206:6. **Index vocabulary and UPDATE-inserted strings sort identically** [P] (same 35 strings, same output). I did not run a mixed test (some strings in the index, others from UPDATE). Source says they share one comparator. Confidence Medium-High.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:209:### 2.2 Evidence: same 35 strings, five locale settings (ORDER BY ?o)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:219:UPDATE-inserted (en_US index with empty vocab)   identical to the en_US line
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:228:### 2.3 Evidence: how the strings reach the comparator
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:231:index vocabulary  : sorted at build time with isLessInTotalWithExternalFlag (TOTAL)   IndexImpl.cpp (sort by unicode order)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:239:### 2.4 Implications
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:241:- **Triplex's exact order must not depend on engine string order.** Keep the existing lexical order-key channels in the history graph (`scalar_order` and the `*_order` fields) for anything that must be codepoint-exact. ORDER BY on projection literals is ICU order, which is fine for human display and not for protocol-level pagination.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:242:- **Pick the locale once, at the first index build.** For a raw-SPARQL user audience, `en_US` with `ignore-punctuation: false` is predictable (spaces and punctuation sort first). `US_POSIX` gives byte order for ASCII-only data, and reverts to root collation for the rest, which can surprise.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:248:## 3. EXISTS, NOT EXISTS, MINUS
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:250:### 3.1 Verdict
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:252:1. **Implementation.** `EXISTS` in `FILTER` or `BIND` is rewritten into an `ExistsJoin`: the argument pattern is planned by a **separate `QueryPlanner`** as if it were its own query, then joined to the outer rows on the variables that both sides expose. The result is a boolean column. `MINUS` is a separate `Minus` operation (sorted merge, with an index-nested-loop variant). [S] `src/engine/ExistsJoin.cpp:239-290 @ 6c5861a`, `src/engine/Filter.cpp:32 @ 6c5861a`, `src/engine/Minus.cpp:21-88 @ 6c5861a`.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:259:### 3.2 Evidence: correlation probe (3 revisions over 2 entities, expect latest-per-entity = {a2, a3})
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:274:### 3.3 Evidence: planner growth (3-triple dataset; group of three patterns plus k `FILTER NOT EXISTS`, each with 2 patterns)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:290:### 3.4 Implications
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:292:- **For projection reads:** do not use correlated `NOT EXISTS` for "latest visible assertion". Use (a) `MINUS` with every correlation variable bound inside the body, (b) `GROUP BY` with `MAX`, or (c) store a pre-computed `current` marker. With the plain projection the common read needs no negation at all.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:298:## 4. Typed literals
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:300:### 4.1 Verdict table (all `[P]` through UPDATE unless marked; source in column 4)
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:304:| `xsd:integer`, `int`, `long`, `short`, `byte`, `unsigned*`, `positiveInteger`, ... | Parsed to int64 then stored in **60 bits**. Range `[-2^59, 2^59-1]` = `[-576460752303423488, 576460752303423487]`. Datatype is erased: results come back as `xsd:int`. | High | `src/global/ValueId.h:105,124,279-283 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/ExportIds.cpp:259-260 @ 6c5861a`, `src/parser/RdfParser.h:166-179 @ 6c5861a` |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:307:| `all-integers-become-doubles` build setting | Source maps it to `OverflowingToDouble`, not `AllToDouble`, so it behaves like the overflow-only mode (likely bug). | Medium | `src/index/IndexImpl.cpp:1706-1710 @ 6c5861a` |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:308:| `xsd:decimal`, `xsd:double`, `xsd:float` | All stored as a double with **49 significant mantissa bits** (low 4 bits dropped). Exported in JSON as `xsd:decimal` with `%.13g`: 13 significant digits. `DATATYPE(?v)` says `xsd:double`. Examples: `0.30000000000000004` exports `0.3`; `1.0000000000000002` and `1.0000000000000004` both `1.0`; `123.456789012345678` exports `123.4567890123`; `9007199254740993` decimal exports `9007199254740992.0`; `123456789012345678901234567890` exports `123456789012345431587114975232.0`; `1e308` exports a 309-digit integer string; `-0.0` survives; NaN and INF export as `xsd:double`. | High | `src/global/ValueId.h:263-270 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/ExportIds.cpp:222-256 @ 6c5861a` |
