lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:420:## 7. Results and paging
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:422:### 7.1 Verdict
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:424:1. **ORDER BY with LIMIT/OFFSET.** Semantics are standard: sort, then offset and limit. The sort is `std::sort` or a parallel sort (`USE_PARALLEL_SORT`, default 3 threads) on the **sort keys only**, so rows with equal keys come out in an unspecified order. [S] `src/index/IdTableUtils.h:33-65 @ 6c5861a`, `src/engine/OrderBy.cpp:66-122 @ 6c5861a`.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:432:### 7.2 Published query performance at 100M to 1B+ triples
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:441:## 8. Scale estimate: 100M facts on 6 CPUs and 12 GiB
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:445:| | **R** reified (current) | **C** compact assertion | **P** plain projection | **P + C** (proposed) |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:453:| `rebuild-index` at 2 CPUs measured rate (2.5M triples/s on R, vocabulary-bound for new words) | ~20 min | ~5 min | ~1 to 2 min | ~6 to 7 min |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:456:| Idle server RSS | 46 MB at 12M triples, larger indexes untested | | | |
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:462:- **Rebuild cadence for P + C:** a fact write adds ~9 to 10 delta triples. With a 1M delta ceiling that is a rebuild every ~100k fact writes. At 10 facts per second that is every ~2.8 hours. Each rebuild takes ~6 to 7 minutes at the measured 2-CPU rate, scaled to P + C. The default rebuild thread settings are deliberately low, so 6 CPUs may not shorten it much. The old index keeps serving meanwhile (CPU and disk are shared).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:463:- **Further savings, not measured here:** `--only-pso-and-pos-permutations` drops four permutations for the history graph if it is never queried by variable predicate (expected disk -60%, build faster, but one flag per index, so it needs its own server or a rule that raw users must not hit it). `--encode-as-id` (IRIs with a numeric tail become ids, no vocabulary entry, ordering between encoded and plain IRIs is wrong) only helps entity IRIs of the form `<prefix><digits>`. `--iri-as-blank-node-regexes` is experimental.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:468:## Top 5 design-relevant facts
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:470:1. **Update cost is O(total delta) per request, with no rollback.** Every update rewrites the whole persisted delta, deep-copies it for the snapshot and clears the result cache. Measured 0.7 s per request at an empty delta rising to 1.5 s at 1M delta, ~0.9 s of which is independent of batch size. A failed multi-operation request and a timed-out single operation both leak partial state into the next published snapshot (and the multi-op leak is persisted). Design: one operation per request, big batches, rebuild at ~1M delta triples, restart on any update error.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:472:3. **The layout is the scale lever.** The current reified layout is 29.9 triples and 724 B of index per fact, 3 billion triples and ~72 GB at 100M facts. Plain projection plus a 7-triple compact assertion graph is ~0.8 billion triples and ~17 GB, with a 3x to 5x faster build and rebuild. Rebuilds from the live index take minutes, not hours.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:473:4. **Typed literals are lossy in specific, silent ways.** Integers wrap at ±2^59 on UPDATE, doubles keep 49 mantissa bits and print 13 digits, date-times lose sub-millisecond and non-hour zones and compare by local clock, invalid typed literals silently become strings. The projection can use native types only inside those ranges, and the exact forms stay in the history graph.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:474:5. **String order is ICU, and EXISTS correlation differs from the spec.** ORDER BY and range FILTER on strings use en_US collation (lowercase-first, case-insensitive primary). Outer variables used only in an inner FILTER are unbound in EXISTS (wrong answers, reproduced), and MINUS must bind its correlation variables inside its body. No RDF-star, so valid and recorded time live on assertion nodes. Default graph is the union of all graphs, so user SPARQL needs `FROM <urn:triplex:current>`.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:478:## Open questions needing experiments
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:480:1. **Delta ceiling at scale.** What does a 1M-triple delta cost on a 100M-fact P+C index, per request (tiny update, 1k-triple update, 25k-triple update), and is the ~0.9 s fixed cost really O(delta) for single-triple requests? Direct measurement of tiny-update latency vs delta size was not done (inferred from the timing breakdown).
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:481:2. **Update-error recovery.** After a timeout mid-mutation, does `rebuild-index` copy the partial permutations into the new index? Does a restart always clear the junk, and is there any case where junk is persisted? Does the 429 on a timed-out single operation always indicate partial application, or only after located triples were added?
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:483:4. **Mixed vocabulary order.** ORDER BY and FILTER ranges when half the strings are in the index vocabulary and half are local. Source says one comparator; not probed.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:485:6. **Greedy planning quality.** Effect of `query-planning-budget=1` on the repo's 142-task packet and on realistic join-heavy reads over the 100M-fact P+C index.
lab/research/R2-qlever-capabilities.md:486:7. **Real latency at 100M facts on 6 CPU, 12 GiB.** Cold and warm latency for entity lookup, attribute range scan, GROUP BY and `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` on a 17 GB index with 12 GiB RAM; startup RSS for a multi-GB vocabulary; `--no-patterns` cost for predicate-count queries at that size.
