    31	## 1. Updates
    32	
    33	### 1.1 Verdict
    34	
    35	1. **Operations.** `INSERT DATA`, `DELETE DATA`, `DELETE WHERE`, `DELETE/INSERT WHERE` (with `WITH` and `USING`), `LOAD`, `CLEAR`, `DROP`, `ADD`, `MOVE`, `COPY` work. `CREATE` is a no-op because empty graphs are not recorded. The Graph Store Protocol (GET, PUT, POST, DELETE, TSOP) also works. [S] `src/parser/sparqlParser/SparqlQleverVisitor.cpp:662-720, 783-945 @ 6c5861a`; [W] https://docs.qlever.dev/update/. Every update response carries the warning `"SPARQL 1.1 Update for QLever is experimental."` [P]
    36	2. **Default graph.** A query without `FROM` reads the **union of all graphs, deduplicated**. `GRAPH ?g { }` ranges over named graphs only (the built-in default graph is excluded unless `treat-default-graph-as-named-graph=true`). [S] `src/engine/QueryPlanner.cpp:2927-2938 @ 6c5861a`, `src/index/CompressedRelation.cpp:292-360 @ 6c5861a`, `src/global/RuntimeParameters.h:182-185 @ 6c5861a`; [P] below.
    37	3. **Delta model.** Updates live in a per-permutation, per-block sorted structure ("located triples") that is merged into scans. Memory is 0.4 KB per delta triple when terms already exist, 1.2 to 1.7 KB when each triple brings new IRIs and literals. Not counted by `-m`. [S][P]
    38	4. **Cost per request.** Every request, however small, redoes O(delta) work: metadata update, full rewrite of the persisted file, deep copy of all six permutations' located triples. [S][P]
    39	5. **Compaction.** `cmd=rebuild-index` rebuilds from the live permutations plus delta (no export, no re-sort), then swaps the new index in without a restart. There is also `--rebuild-index-strategy automatic:min:max:fraction`. [S][P][W]
    40	6. **Atomicity.** A multi-operation request runs on one update thread and publishes one snapshot at the end. There is **no rollback** on failure or timeout. [S][P] Measured below.
    41	7. **Version token.** None. `cmd=get-index-id` returns a constant (`#.1.1.1.1`), responses carry no ETag. [P]
    42	
    43	### 1.2 Evidence: graph semantics (probe P1, default graph plus `G1` and `G2`)
    44	
    45	Setup: `INSERT DATA { <s1> <p> "v" . GRAPH <G1> { <s1> <p> "v" . <s2> <p> "w" } GRAPH <G2> { <s1> <p> "v" . <s3> <p> "x" } }` (5 triples).
    46	
    47	| Query | Result |
    48	|-------|--------|
    49	| `SELECT ?s ?o WHERE { ?s <p> ?o }` (no FROM) | `s1 "v"`, `s2 "w"`, `s3 "x"`. `COUNT(*)` = 3. `s1 "v"` is in three graphs and appears once. |
    50	| `SELECT ?g ?s ?o WHERE { GRAPH ?g { ?s <p> ?o } }` | G1 and G2 rows only (4 rows). The default-graph copy is not returned. |
    51	| `... FROM <G1> ...` | `s1`, `s2` |
    52	| `... FROM <G1> FROM <G2> ...` | `s1`, `s2`, `s3` |
    53	| `... FROM NAMED <G2> ... GRAPH ?g` | G2 rows only |
    54	
    55	**Consequence for a "current" graph plus a "history" graph on one server.** A plain `SELECT * { ?s ?p ?o }` sees both. 0.6.0 has no server-level default-graph setting. Raw SPARQL users must write `FROM <urn:triplex:current>`, or a proxy must inject it, or the two graphs live on two servers. [S][P]
    56	
    57	**Graph metadata limit.** Block metadata records at most 20 distinct graphs per block (`src/index/ConstantsIndexBuilding.h:108 @ 6c5861a`). Beyond that a block has no graph info and `FROM` or `GRAPH <g>` filtering is a per-row filter. Two or three graphs stay cheap. One graph per scope (thousands) does not. [S]
