   475	#### 4.3.4 The plain triple, the class, and the assertion node
   476	
   477	Per assertion, additive to what is written today:
   478	
   479	```trig
   480	# scope graph <urn:triplex:scope:dGVzdA>   (existing field triples, plus one new)
   481	<urn:triplex:assertion:QUJD>
   482	    field:entity        entity:person:maria ;          # NEW, IRI-valued, joins to the plain triple
   483	    field:entity_id     "person:maria" ;               # existing, literal
   484	    field:attribute     ":person/name" ;               # existing
   485	    field:value_type    "string" ;  field:value_string "Maria" ;
   486	    field:valid_from    "1767225600000" ;  field:recorded_position "42" ;
   487	    ... .                                              # existing columns and numeric shadows
   488	
   489	# assertion graph <urn:triplex:assertion:QUJD>   (NEW, two triples)
   490	GRAPH <urn:triplex:assertion:QUJD> {
   491	    entity:person:maria  person:name  "Maria" ;
   492	                         a            type:Person .    # only when the assertion has an entity type
   493	}
   494	```
   495	
   496	- **Assertion identity** is the graph IRI. It is also the subject of the engine fields, so a join on `?a` links the plain triple to its validity, recording and retraction.
   497	- **Reserved namespaces** (`:_tx/`, `:triplex/`, `_triplex/` entities) are not published as plain triples by default, since the journal envelope is stored as ordinary assertions (141 triples for one user assertion in R4's count).
   498	- **Added volume** is three triples and one graph per published assertion, against roughly two dozen triples per assertion today (counted from `rowFields`, not measured on an index). The one-graph-per-assertion cost at 100 million assertions is unmeasured (P3).
   499	
   500	**A SPARQL author's view.**
   501	
   502	```sparql
   503	PREFIX person:  <urn:triplex:attr:person:>
   504	PREFIX company: <urn:triplex:attr:company:>
   505	
   506	SELECT ?name ?company WHERE {
   507	  GRAPH ?a1 { ?p person:name     ?name }  ${visible("?a1")}
   508	  GRAPH ?a2 { ?p person:employer ?c    }   ${visible("?a2")}
   509	  GRAPH ?a3 { ?c company:name    ?company } ${visible("?a3")}
   510	}
   511	```
   512	
   513	`visible("?a")` expands to the existing basis filter block that `NativeContext.visible()` already writes (recorded position cut, valid interval, retraction), applied to the assertion node in the scope graph [repo NativeContext.ts:76-90]. A SPARQL reader sees plain triples in graphs and an obvious filter. The trace shows the expanded text.
   514	
   515	`CONSTRUCT { ?s ?p ?o } WHERE { GRAPH ?a { ?s ?p ?o } visible(?a) }` exports a plain RDF dataset at any basis. That export is the interoperability path and is exact.
   516	
   517	#### 4.3.5 What is deliberately not done
   518	
   519	- **No bare-pattern read.** A bare `{ ?p person:name ?n }` (outside `GRAPH`, no `FROM`) in QLever reads across graphs: outside `GRAPH` clauses the active default graphs are used, and unconstrained means all graphs `[clone references/qlever/src/engine/QueryPlanner.h:31-36, 776-778; src/parser/DatasetClauses.h:44-48]` (read, not run, P4). It returns every assertion ever made, for every tenant.
   520	- **No `FROM <scope>` injection.** With `FROM` and no `FROM NAMED` the dataset's named-graph set is empty under SPARQL 1.1 section 13.2 (QLever's `DatasetClauses.h:44-48` mirrors the default-graph half of that rule; the named-graph half is part of P4), so the assertion graphs would vanish. Isolation comes from the join to the scope graph inside `visible()`. A bare `GRAPH ?a { ... }` still reads other tenants' plain triples, so physical tenant isolation (one QLever index per tenant) or a host-level boundary stays necessary. Authorization is host-owned in Triplex already `[repo docs/agents.md, anti-pattern table]`.
   521	- **No published "head" graph yet.** It needs a staleness mechanism that follows write commits and valid-time boundaries (`nextTemporalBoundary`). Decide after P3 (D2).
