   111	Row shape: `TripleRow` (`packages/core/src/Triple.ts:76-98`), 20 columns, snake_case, numbers as JS numbers, `value_boolean` as 0/1.
   112	
   113	### 2.2 How one Triplex `transact` calls the adapter
   114	
   115	`TriplesLive.transact`, `packages/core/src/store/TriplesLive.ts:253-523`. The order matters because it fixes what each adapter must support.
   116	
   117	```
   118	triples.transact(ops, meta)                              TriplesLive.ts:253
   119	  |  structuredClone(ops, meta)                          :254-273
   120	  v
   121	adapter.withTransaction(effect, {replaySafe:true})       :275, :521
   122	  |  reject unsupported value families / reserved names  :277-292
   123	  |  validatePreconditions (TripleLive must have a matching retract)  :293-300
   124	  |  validate commandId length 1..1024                   :301-307
   125	  |  txId = nextTxId, timestamp = now                    :308-309
   126	  |  adapter.claimCommand(commandId, txId, ts)           :310-325  -> CommandAlreadyCommittedError if non-null
   127	  |  position = adapter.nextCommitPosition()             :326
   128	  |  EntityState preconditions: adapter.getByEntity(id)  :330-346  -> TransactionConflictError on mismatch
   129	  |  meta.enforce: adapter.validateConstraints? else portable Constraint.newlyViolated  :348-410
   130	  |  for each op, in order:
   131	  |     assert*  -> batched into adapter.batchInsert / insert        :209-251 (flushAssertBatch)
   132	  |     retract  -> adapter.getById, adapter.retract; TripleLive precondition -> TransactionConflictError  :432-466
   133	  |     retract-pattern -> adapter.query(pattern), adapter.retract per row  :471-495
   134	  |  journal: for each metadataInputs(): adapter.insert (entityType "_Transaction")  :514-517
   135	  v
   136	adapter commits everything staged in one backend step (XTDB: one XTDB tx; QLever: one SPARQL UPDATE)
   137	```
   138	
   139	**Journal rows are ordinary assertions.** `metadataInputs` (`packages/core/src/store/transactionMetadata.ts:29-57`) emits, per Triplex transaction, as `_Transaction` entity `txId`:
   140	
   141	| Journal assertion | Attribute (`utils/id.ts:25-39`) | Count |
   142	| --- | --- | --- |
   143	| position | `:_tx/position` (number) | 1 |
   144	| instant | `:_tx/instant` (datetime) | 1 |
   145	| actor, command id, correlation id, causation id, config snapshot | `:_tx/actor` ... `:_tx/config-snapshot` (string) | 1 each, only when set in `meta` |
   146	| changed entity | `:_tx/changed-entity` (ref) | 1 per distinct entity |
   147	| change | `:_tx/change` (json, full change record including the value) | 1 per assert or retract |
   148	
   149	Read paths for the journal use `adapter.query({entityType:"_Transaction"})` (`TriplesLive.ts:634-670`), so a native layer reading journals sees the same rows.
   150	
   151	### 2.3 CAS and preconditions
   152	
   153	- **Position CAS lives in the adapter.** The core never compares positions. Each adapter makes commit conditional on the committed position still equaling the position read at transaction start (`sourceK`). A mismatch raises `XtdbSerializationConflict`/`QleverSerializationConflict`, and `withTransaction` replays the callback up to `maxRetries` (default 12) only when `replaySafe` is true. `TriplesLive` always passes `replaySafe:true` (`:521`), so data-only commands retry automatically.
   154	- **Triplex-level preconditions are read-time checks inside the same transaction.** `TripleLive` fails when `adapter.retract` returns `false` (`TriplesLive.ts:432-466`). `EntityState` compares sorted triple ids from `getByEntity` with the expected set (`:330-346`). Because the position CAS covers the whole scope, a concurrent commit that changes any fact in the scope aborts the stale attempt even when the precondition itself would still hold.
   155	- **Consequence for the 256-client write story.** The CAS is one hot row per scope (XTDB `tx_meta` row, QLever `<urn:triplex:meta>` position triple). Concurrent writers to one scope conflict and retry. An in-process `admissionQueue` semaphore (`XtdbStorageAdapter.ts:85, 422-426`; `QleverStorageAdapter.ts:86, 360-364`) serializes within one process.
   156	
   157	### 2.4 How Datalog reaches a backend
   158	
   159	`QueryExecutor` service, `packages/core/src/storage/QueryExecutor.ts:115-166`: `execute`, `executePage`, `explain`, `explainPage`. `TriplesLive.queryAll/queryPage` (`TriplesLive.ts:688-740`) resolve the temporal basis (adding `recordedPosition` via `adapter.currentCommitPosition()`), validate the query, then call `executor.execute(query, debug, basis)`.
   160	
   161	| Backend | Executor | Strategy |
   162	| --- | --- | --- |
   163	| XTDB | `packages/xtdb/src/XtdbQueryExecutor.ts` (276 lines), `src/query/compiler.ts`, `recursion.ts` | Datalog AST to scoped XTDB SQL. Recursive rules run as server-side rounds into `tx_query_relations` because XTDB 2.2.0 rejects recursive CTEs (`native/Reachability.ts:30-34`). |
   164	| QLever | `packages/qlever/src/QleverQueryExecutor.ts` (446 lines), `compiler.ts` (1013 lines), `recursion.ts` | Datalog AST to SPARQL over the reified graph. Includes optional "plan" profiles (aggregate, negation, source, retirement, scalar order) selected by constructor options (`QleverQueryOptions`). |
   165	| KV (oracle) | `packages/core/src/kv/` | Hexastore scans plus nested-loop joins. `KvTriples.layer` is the in-memory reference runtime. |
   166	
   167	Each backend's runtime is `Runtime.define({name, storage, queries})` (`packages/xtdb/src/index.ts:68`, `packages/qlever/src/index.ts:65`). `.layer({scope, capabilities})` binds a `DatabaseScope`. Scope is a JSON tuple `["triplex-scope-v1", env, tenant, database, generation]` (`packages/core/src/runtime/DatabaseScope.ts:22-29`), and `DatabaseScope.test(name)` builds a throwaway one.
   168	
   169	---
   170	
   270	### 4.1 Physical graph layout
   271	
   272	One **named graph per scope**: `<urn:triplex:scope:BASE64URL(scopeKey)>` (`rdf.ts:46`). Subjects and predicates (`rdf.ts:46-51`):
   273	
   274	| Subject IRI | Predicates | Meaning |
   275	| --- | --- | --- |
   276	| `<urn:triplex:assertion:B64(id)>` | `<urn:triplex:field:COLUMN>` for every non-null column, plus shadows (below) | One reified assertion. |
   277	| `<urn:triplex:claim:B64(commandId)>` | `field:command_id, tx_id, recorded_at, position` (4) | Command claim. |
   278	| `<urn:triplex:operation:B64(operationId)>` | `field:operation_id, attempt_id, position` (3) | Commit receipt marker. |
   279	| `<urn:triplex:meta>` | `field:position, field:position_native` (2) | Scope position CAS cell. Rewritten in place every commit (DELETE old, INSERT new). |
   280	
   281	Objects are RDF string literals (lexical channel), except `*_native` shadows, which are native numbers or `xsd:double` literals. Scratch graphs: `<urn:triplex:native:B64(scope):runId>` plus `<urn:triplex:native-receipts:...>` for native reachability (`native/BasisRelations.ts:114-118`) and `urn:triplex:derived:` nodes for Datalog recursion (`recursion.ts:105`).
   282	
   283	### 4.2 Triples per assertion
   284	
   285	Counts come from the real `rowFields` (`rdf.ts:202-259`) and were confirmed end to end by capturing the SPARQL UPDATE of real `Triples.transact` calls through a stubbed `fetch` (`R4-test-outputs/qlever-triple-count.mts`, output `qlever-triple-count.out.txt`).
   286	
   287	Per assertion row:
   288	
   289	| Row type | Triples | Composition |
   290	| --- | --- | --- |
   291	| string / ref / json / blob, no `valid_to`, no `created_by` | **27** | 4 summary (`_row` JSON blob, `scalar_family`, `scalar_lexical`, `scalar_order`) + 5 value/identity (`id, entity_id, attribute, value_type, value_string`) + 4 each for `recorded_at, recorded_position, valid_from, schema_version` (value, `_order`, `_exact`, `_native`) + `entity_type` + `tx_id`. |
   292	| number / datetime / boolean | **30** (29 when a `number` is not exactly representable, because `value_number_native` is omitted) | 27 base, with the numeric value column carrying its 4-triple group (value, `_order`, `_exact`, `_native`) instead of 1 triple. |
   293	| int64 ("integer") | **32** | 27 base, plus `value_integer_order, _exact, _sum_hi, _sum_lo, _native`. |
   294	| `created_by` set | +1 | |
   295	| `valid_to` set | +4 | |
   296	| retracted row | +9 | `retracted_at` (4), `retracted_position` (4), `retract_tx_id` (1). The retraction rewrites the same subject through DELETE/INSERT. |
   297	
   298	Per Triplex transaction (all inside one UPDATE):
   299	
   300	| Item | Triples |
   301	| --- | --- |
   302	| Data assertions | per table above |
   303	| Journal assertions (`_Transaction`) | position 30, instant 30, +27 per meta string set (actor, command id, correlation, causation, config snapshot), +27 per distinct changed entity, +27 per change |
   304	| Operation marker | 3 (kept forever) |
   305	| Command claim | 4 (only with `commandId`) |
   306	| `<urn:triplex:meta>` | 2 (replaced, so net growth 0 after the first commit) |
   307	
   308	**Measured** triples in one SPARQL UPDATE (data + journal + claim + operation + meta):
   309	
   310	| Transaction | Total | Breakdown |
   311	| --- | --- | --- |
   312	| 1 string assert, no meta | **146** | 141 assertion + 3 operation + 2 meta |
   313	| 1 string assert + `commandId` + `actor` | **205** | 196 assertion + 4 claim + 3 operation + 2 meta |
   314	| 5 string asserts on 1 entity, no meta | **362** | 357 assertion + 3 + 2 |
   315	| 1 number assert, no meta | **149** | 144 assertion + 3 + 2 |
   316	
   317	Closed form for string-typed assertions, no meta: `54*A + 27*E + 63` new triples, where `A` is the assertion count and `E` the distinct entities (the `+63` is 60 for position/instant plus 3 for the operation marker). **The journal doubles the data cost.** At the dense load profile (500 assertions and 50 entities per command, from `packages/qualification/load/README.md`) this is 28,413 triples, about 56.8 per fact, so 100 million active facts is roughly **5.7 billion triples**. At the sparse profile (1 fact per entity, 500 entities per command) it is about 81 per fact. Both are computed from the formula, not measured at scale. `lab/PLAN.md` section 2.1 estimates 1.5 to 2 billion; that figure matches the data rows alone and omits the journal.
   318	
   319	### 4.3 Writes: one conditional UPDATE, no engine transaction
   320	
   321	`persist` (`QleverStorageAdapter.ts:187-278`) builds a single request:
   322	
   323	```
   324	DELETE { GRAPH g { ?s ?p ?old } }
   325	INSERT { GRAPH g { ?s ?p ?new } }
   326	WHERE  {
   327	  { SELECT * WHERE {                                           <- guard subquery  :228-229
   328	      OPTIONAL { GRAPH g { meta field:position ?sourcePosition } }
   329	      FILTER(COALESCE(?sourcePosition,"0") = "<sourceK>")                          :217  position CAS
   330	      FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH g { operation:<id> field:operation_id ?x } }       :218  duplicate attempt
   331	      FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH g { claim:<cmd> field:command_id ?x } }  per claim :220-223
   332	      FILTER NOT EXISTS { VALUES ?n {new ids} GRAPH g { ?n field:id ?x } }         :224-227  identity reuse
   333	  } }
   334	  VALUES (?s ?p ?new) { <every triple of every row, claim, operation, meta> }      :194-215, :230
   335	  OPTIONAL { GRAPH g { ?s ?p ?old } }
   336	}
   337	```
   338	
   339	If any guard fails the WHERE yields no rows and the UPDATE inserts nothing, so "all or nothing" holds for guard failures. The engine provides no rollback for execution errors: `packages/qlever/README.md` ("`--persist-updates` writes the complete native delta snapshot...") records that source review found unpublished delta mutation before persistence without exception rollback, and that a live counterexample showed a failed multi-operation request retaining its first INSERT. The adapter therefore uses one MODIFY, and failure atomicity stays unqualified.
   340	
   341	### 4.4 Positions, claims, outcome reconciliation
   342	
   343	| Concern | Mechanism | Lines |
   344	| --- | --- | --- |
   345	| Position read | `SELECT ?position WHERE { GRAPH g { <urn:triplex:meta> field:position ?position } }` at the start of the outermost transaction. | `:93-102, :327-331` |
   346	| Position allocation | In-memory increment from `sourceK`; persisted by the UPDATE. | `:416-426` |
   347	| Claims | Staged in memory; read check filters `xsd:integer(?position) <= sourceK`; barrier is the `FILTER NOT EXISTS` plus the claim subject IRI. | `:429-450, :220-223` |
   348	| Retraction | Reads the row (`queryRows` by id), stages a copy with retraction fields. | `:458-474` |
   349	| Nested savepoints | Same copy-on-enter scheme as XTDB. | `:286-326` |
   350	| Retry | `QleverSerializationConflict`, `replaySafe`, `maxRetries` 12. | `:343-359` |
   351	| Reconcile after UPDATE | Poll `SELECT ?attempt` for the operation marker every 25 ms up to `resolutionTimeoutMs` (30 s). Marker present: committed. Marker absent but UPDATE acknowledged: re-read the position; if it differs from `sourceK` raise `QleverSerializationConflict`, else raise `WriteError` (a claim or identity guard rejected it). UPDATE errored or timed out and no marker by the deadline: `TransactionOutcomeUnknown`. | `:231-277` |
   352	| Resolve later | `resolveQleverOperation` reads the marker by operation id. | `Operations.ts:16-37` |
   353	
   354	### 4.5 Managed writer
   355	
   356	The "managed writer" is **not wired into the adapter**. It is a private Python experiment in `experiments/qlever-managed-writer/` (`broker.py` 347 lines, `native_worker.py` 212, `native_faults.py` 419, `DESIGN.md`, `NATIVE-PACKET.md`). Its design admits one prepared MODIFY at a time across all scopes under an `fcntl.flock` global lock, keeps an append-only hash-chained intent journal, and after any uncertain response terminates the owned server, fsyncs the persisted snapshot, restarts and verifies the marker. `README.md` states "No native trial has run" and goal 7 (atomic writes and recovery) is open. Offline tests: `python3 experiments/qlever-managed-writer/run_tests.py` (writes to `.qlever/managed-model/`, so not run here).
   357	
   358	### 4.6 Restart and persistence
   359	
   360	- The server is started with `--persist-updates` (`qlever-start.sh:9`; `compose.qlever.yaml` runs that script). Updates are held in a native delta; with persistence the whole delta snapshot is written to a temporary file and renamed before the next reader snapshot is published (`packages/qlever/README.md`, `--persist-updates` paragraph). Cost per update grows with all retained delta triples. At 146 triples per minimal transaction this is the dominant scale risk.
   361	- First start builds an index from a one-triple bootstrap file (`qlever-start.sh:4-8`). Later starts reuse `.qlever/data/triplex.index.*` plus the persisted updates file.
   362	- Observed in the README: clean restart and recovery after a read-triggered OOM pass narrower checks; power-loss durability and cancellation during an UPDATE are unqualified.
   363	- `compose.qlever.yaml` uses `--no-access-check` and no token. The homebase entry scripts require a token file (section 7), ours does not.
