   221	### 5.3 Why hex order keys sort in codepoint order under QLever's comparator
   222	
   223	**Claim.** For strings over an alphabet Σ of digits and lowercase letters, `TripleComponentComparator::compare(TOTAL)` equals unsigned lexicographic order with a proper prefix first. Fixed width per codepoint then makes key order equal codepoint order.
   224	
   225	**Why, step by step.**
   226	
   227	1. **Datatype class.** Both keys are plain literals starting with `"`, so step 1 of the comparator ties (`StringSortComparator.h:311-314`).
   228	2. **Primary weights.** In the ICU root collation that `en_US` uses, every character of `[0-9a-z]` has its own primary weight, digits sort before letters, and letters sort alphabetically. None are variable, ignorable or part of a contraction. So the primary key is the concatenation of per-character weights.
   229	3. **Prefixes.** A shorter string's sort key ends at the level separator, which is below every primary weight, so a proper prefix sorts first. That equals bytewise order.
   230	4. **No lower-level effect.** Without accents or uppercase there is nothing at secondary or tertiary level, and the IDENTICAL level (NFD codepoint compare) and the byte tie-break only act when ICU already says equal, which cannot happen for different strings.
   231	5. **Option defaults.** Numeric collation and alternate-shifted handling are off for `en_US` with `ignore-punctuation=false` (`IndexImpl.cpp:1618-1648`). The recorded locale in the probe index is `{language:"en", country:"US", ignore-punctuation:false}`.
   232	6. **Fixed width.** 6 hex digits cover U+0000 to U+10FFFF. With equal width per codepoint, comparing keys position by position compares codepoints, and a prefix of the codepoint sequence is a prefix of the key. The code points are taken as Unicode scalar values, as `wellFormed()` in `rdf.ts` already requires.
   233	
   234	**What the probe adds.** Empirical proof under the pinned binary for the alphabet and for both vocabulary and local-vocabulary words (T2, T3, base-36 test). The negative control (base-62 with uppercase) fails, which shows the test can fail.
   235	
   236	**The repository already relies on this property.** `numericOrderKey` and `integerOrderKey` are fixed-width lowercase hex (`rdf.ts:65-76`) and the compiler compares them with SPARQL `<`, `>` (`compiler.ts:373-377`, `NativeContext.ts:87-89`). A break in the property would already have broken the temporal cut filters, so the 142 packets corroborate it indirectly. The probe is the first direct test.
   237	
   238	**How ranges and pages use the key.**
   239	
   240	- **Native fact.** Add `physicalType("text")` an `order` channel (`NativeContext.ts:51-52` has none, and line 103 throws if one is requested) and have `rowFields` write it (`rdf.ts:220-231`).
   241	- **Order.** `ORDER BY ?labelKey ?entityKey`, then `LIMIT`/`OFFSET` at the captured cut, as in the existing page recipe. Entity tie-breakers need their own key if codepoint tie-break order matters.
   242	- **Range.** `FILTER(?labelKey >= "<enc(lo)>" && ?labelKey < "<enc(hi)>")`, with `enc` the same TypeScript function. A "starts with p" range is `[enc(p), enc(p) + "g")`, because `g` sorts above every hex digit.
   243	- **Keyset.** `FILTER(?labelKey > :last || (?labelKey = :last && ?entityKey > :lastEntity))`.
   244	- **Datalog.** `scalar()` returns `order: text` for strings (`compiler.ts:103`) and `rowFields` stores `scalarOrder = scalarLexical` (`rdf.ts:220-225`). Switching the string branch to the key is two lines plus the persisted-scalar admission check (`PersistedScalarSource.ts`), but existing persisted `scalar_order` values for strings would need a backfill, so a new predicate name is safer.
   245	
   246	**Cost notes.**
   247	
   248	- Storage was measured on a synthetic 200k-assertion corpus with two triples each. The Triplex layout writes about 30 to 35 triples per assertion (my count of `rowFields`, not a measured index), so the relative cost against a full Triplex index is smaller than in the probe. Measure it on the real layout before quoting a percentage.
   249	- A base-36 key (4 characters per codepoint) did not save storage. 59.3 B versus 57.7 B per assertion, because the vocabulary compresses hex well. Keep hex.
   250	- Long text costs 6 key characters per codepoint in the vocabulary. A cap would break exactness, so none is proposed.
   251	
   252	### 5.4 Exact equivalence conditions for the authoring rewrites
   253	
   254	Let `O` be the outer group, μ one of its solutions, and `P` the group inside `NOT EXISTS { P }`.
   255	
   256	- **QLever 0.6.0 semantic.** Keep μ iff no row of `P`, evaluated alone, is compatible with μ on the variables `P` itself binds. Filters inside `P` see only those variables.
   257	- **Spec semantic.** Keep μ iff `P` with μ substituted has no solution.
   258	
   259	**C1. `FILTER NOT EXISTS { P }` equals `FILTER NOT EXISTS { B . P }` under both semantics** when all hold.
   260	
   261	1. Every outer variable `x` that occurs free in `P` (in a `FILTER`, `BIND`, aggregate, nested group or sub-select) is bound inside `B`, at the same group level as the expression that uses it.
   262	2. `B` is a copy of the conjunct of `O` that bound `x`, built from the same fact helper so it carries the same graph, cut, valid-time and retirement filters. μ therefore satisfies `B`.
   263	3. Variables of `B` that are not outer variables are renamed apart.
   264	4. Each correlated variable is bound in every outer solution. If it can be UNDEF (from `OPTIONAL`, or an error in `BIND`), the spec treats the variable as free while QLever's join treats UNDEF as a wildcard, and the rewrite is not guaranteed.
   265	5. When `P` has `OPTIONAL`, `UNION` or `MINUS` inside, `B` must appear in every branch or group that uses `x`.
   266	
   267	Duplicates do not matter, because `EXISTS` is a boolean.
   268	
   269	**C2. `NOT EXISTS` equals `MINUS`** when the C1 conditions hold, the set of variables shared between `B . P` and `O` is non-empty, and every shared variable is definitely bound in every outer solution. Without a shared variable `MINUS` removes nothing (N6, `QueryPlanner.cpp:2952`). The multiplicity of surviving outer rows is unchanged.
   270	
   271	**C4. `NOT EXISTS` equals `OPTIONAL { B . P } FILTER(!BOUND(?sentinel))`** when C1 holds and `?sentinel` is a variable of `P` that is always bound when `P` matches and does not occur in `O`.
   272	
   273	**C3. "No later row" equals "my key reaches the group maximum"** when the order is the exact key channel (a strict total preorder), the group key is identical in outer and inner, and either the outer row belongs to the inner relation (use `=`) or the aggregate is joined with `OPTIONAL` and tested as `!BOUND(?mx) || ?key >= ?mx` (S6). Ties stay (both tied maxima survive), which matches strict `>` in `NOT EXISTS`.
   274	
   304	## 8. Limits of this report
   305	
   306	- **Probe scope.** One 5,205-triple index plus one 200k-triple storage sample. Hex-key order was verified for strings of 1 to 3 characters exhaustively and up to 30 characters randomly, not for arbitrary lengths. The ICU argument in 5.3 covers arbitrary length, and the probe gate in E-S1 guards a future image change.
   307	- **Not measured.** NoICU behavior (no binary), build time and RAM, storage on the real 30 to 35 triple layout, backfill throughput, the planner-memory effect of re-bind.
   308	- **Upstream claims** about SPARQL 1.2 changing `EXISTS` come from the cited issue thread, not from the 1.2 draft, which I did not read.
   309	- **Probe hygiene.** One throwaway container `tx2-probe-r5` on `DOCKER_HOST=unix:///Users/sam/.colima/triplex2/docker.sock`, `--cpus 2 --memory 3g`, no bind mounts (the scratch directory is not mounted into the VM, so data lived in the container filesystem), removed with `docker rm -f -v`. No repository source was modified. `/Users/sam/projects/triplex-db` and ports 15442 and 17019 were not touched. No personal data was sent in any web request (only public GitHub issue reads through `gh`).
