   323	### 4.2 Implications for the plain projection
   324	
   325	| Triplex value | Native projection literal | Verdict |
   326	|---------------|--------------------------|---------|
   327	| string | plain literal | exact (but ICU-ordered) |
   328	| boolean | `xsd:boolean` | exact |
   329	| integer within ±2^59 | `xsd:integer` | exact, returned as `xsd:int`, safe for `FILTER`, `ORDER BY`, aggregates (sums wrap past 2^59) |
   330	| int64 beyond ±2^59, bigint | custom typed or plain string literal in the projection, exact lexical form in the history graph | native types wrap silently, so never emit them |
   331	| number (binary64) | `xsd:double` | display only, 13 digits out, 49-bit mantissa in; exactness stays in the history graph |
   332	| instant / datetime | `xsd:dateTime` in UTC `Z`, millisecond | only if Triplex time is already integer milliseconds in UTC. Never emit offsets other than `Z`, years outside `±9999`, or sub-millisecond values |
   333	| ref | IRI | exact |
   334	| json, blob metadata | string literal | exact |
   335	
   336	The existing order-key and `_exact` shadows in `rdf.ts` exist because of precisely these limits. The projection can drop most of them for supported ranges, and the history graph must keep them.
