   104	## Q2. Valid-time DML: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, ERASE, PATCH
   105	
   106	**Verdict.** Every operation exists in SQL over pgwire, including `PATCH`. XTQL transaction operations (`put-docs`, `patch-docs`, `delete-docs`, `erase-docs`) exist only in JVM clients. `INSERT` is a put that replaces the whole document for its valid period. Merging semantics need `PATCH`.
   107	
   108	**Evidence.**
   109	
   110	| Operation | Semantics observed in 2.2.0 | Source or docs |
   111	| --- | --- | --- |
   112	| `INSERT INTO t (_id, _valid_from, _valid_to, ...)` | Both valid-time columns optional. Default is now (the tx system time) to end of time. Replaces the whole document for that period | `sql/txs.md:28`, probes p2 steps 1-2 |
   113	| `INSERT` where `_valid_from >= _valid_to` | Statement fails at statement time, SQLSTATE `08P01`, "Invalid valid times". Equal bounds also fail | probe p2 steps 12-13 |
   114	| Two `INSERT`s for one `_id` in one tx | Second wins, and only one version exists (same system time) | probe p2 step 15 |
   115	| `UPDATE t FOR PORTION OF VALID_TIME FROM a [TO b] SET ...` | Changes only the `SET` columns inside `[a, b)`, splits slices at `a` and `b`. No effect when no document exists in the portion (no gap fill) | `sql/txs.md:30-47`, probes p2 step 3, p2b |
   116	| `UPDATE` without a portion clause | Now to end of time (documented XTDB deviation from SQL:2011) | `sql/txs.md:46` |
   117	| `UPDATE SET _id/_valid_from` | Rejected at planning, "Cannot UPDATE _id column" | probe p2 steps 10-11 |
   118	| `UPDATE` that would write an identical document | Skipped, no new version (v2.2.0 breaking-change list). `INSERT` of an identical document still creates a new system version | probe p10 section 1 |
   119	| `DELETE FROM t FOR PORTION OF VALID_TIME FROM a TO b` | Removes that slice. `FOR ALL VALID_TIME` removes all valid time. Default is now to end of time | `sql/txs.md:69-84`, probe p2 step 4, p2b |
   120	| `PATCH INTO t [FOR [PORTION OF] VALID_TIME FROM a [TO b]] RECORDS {...}` | Key-level merge: present keys override, **absent or null keys are preserved**. Fills gaps inside the portion with a document holding only the patched keys. Several `PATCH`es in one tx collapse to one version | `Sql.g4:31,823`, `sql/txs.md:50-67`, probes p2 steps 5-7, p2b, p12 |
   121	| `ERASE FROM t WHERE ...` | Removes every version in all valid time and all system time. `FOR ALL SYSTEM_TIME FOR ALL VALID_TIME` then returns zero rows | `sql/txs.md:86-97`, probe p2 step 17 |
   122	
   123	*Overlapping insert, exact behaviour (probe p2 steps 1-2).* Start with `p1` valid `[2024-01-01, 2024-06-01)` holding `name='Ann', email='ann@x'`. Then `INSERT ... (_id, _valid_from, _valid_to, name) VALUES ('p1', 2024-03-01, 2024-04-01, 'Bea')`:
   124	
   125	```
   126	valid time:  01-01      03-01      04-01      06-01
   127	             |  Ann+email |  Bea (email = NULL)  |  Ann+email  |
   128	             ^ original    ^ replaced by INSERT   ^ original, re-split
   129	```
   130	
   131	The inserted document replaced the stored one for its period. The `email` of that slice became `NULL`.
   132	
   133	*Retraction of one attribute for a valid period (probe p12).*
   134	
   135	- `UPDATE ent FOR PORTION OF VALID_TIME FROM 04-01 TO 05-01 SET email = NULL WHERE _id='e1'` set `email` to `NULL` only inside that period and split the row at both ends.
   136	- `PATCH ... RECORDS {_id:'e1', email: null}` over `[05-01, 06-01)` **did not retract**. It preserved the stored value and split the row at `06-01`.
   137	- **Slices are not coalesced.** After that no-op `PATCH`, two adjacent rows with identical column values remained (`[05-01,06-01)` and `[06-01,07-01)`). Row count grows with every distinct boundary.
   138	
   139	*Parameter typing in DML.* An untyped parameter fails with "Missing types for args - client must specify types for all non-null params in DML statements". The repo's `XtdbClient.ts` already avoids this by calling `pool.typed(value, oid)` for every parameter. A json-typed (OID 114) or jsonb-typed (3802) parameter is **stored as text**, not as a struct (probe p9). A transit-typed parameter (OID 16384, JSON-quoted transit text) is stored as a real struct, set, keyword, uuid or instant (probe p9b). A statement carries at most 32,767 parameters ("Value out of range for short: 54000" for 54,000).
   140	
   141	*XTQL as DML.* `docs/src/content/docs/reference/main/xtql/txs.md` documents `put-docs`, `patch-docs` (with `:valid-from` / `:valid-to`), `delete-docs`, `erase-docs` as EDN for `xt/execute-tx` in Clojure. `Sql.g4:704` shows XTQL only as a query expression, so there is no XTQL DML over pgwire.
   142	
   143	**Confidence.** High.
   144	
   145	**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
   146	
   147	- Writing an assertion of attribute A over `[a, b)` maps to `PATCH INTO person FOR VALID_TIME FROM a TO b RECORDS {_id: e, person$name: v}`. It merges into existing slices and splits the row at `a` and `b` automatically.
   148	- Retracting A over `[a, b)` maps to `UPDATE person FOR PORTION OF VALID_TIME FROM a TO b SET person$name = NULL WHERE _id = e`. `PATCH` with `null` cannot retract.
   149	- Creating an entity with a full document maps to `INSERT`, and a later `INSERT` for the same period overwrites every column, so it must not be used for partial attribute updates.
   150	- A Triplex entity with per-attribute intervals produces one row per distinct boundary, and rows are never merged back. Row count per entity equals the number of distinct interval endpoints across all its attributes plus one.
   151	- Triplex valid times are non-negative epoch milliseconds (possibly fractional). Native timestamps accepted `-290000-01-01` to `+294000-01-01` (probe p10), so the domain fits at microsecond resolution (Q3 covers conversion).
   152	
