   155	## Q3. Columns, names and types
   156	
   157	**Verdict.** An attribute key `:person/name` becomes column `person$name`, reachable from XTQL as `person/name` and from SQL as `person$name`. The normalisation is lossy (case-folding, `-` to `_`). Wire types are well behaved for monomorphic columns and surprising for unions, sets, structs and `URI`. Tables get sparse columns on first write with no DDL.
   158	
   159	**Evidence: naming.**
   160	
   161	- `api/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/util/NormalForm.kt:9-14`. `normalForm0` replaces `-` with `_`, maps a leading `xt/` to `_`, splits on `.` `/` `$`, joins with `$`, lowercases. For symbols with a namespace, `NormalForm.kt:33` yields `ns$name`.
   162	- `NormalForm.kt:45-46`. Table keyword `:ns/tbl` becomes schema `ns`, table `tbl` (default schema `public`, `-` becomes `_`, lowercase).
   163	- Probe results:
   164	
   165	| Spelling | Result |
   166	| --- | --- |
   167	| SQL `INSERT INTO nsy (_id, person$name)` unquoted | Works, column `person$name` |
   168	| SQL `"first-name"`, `"person.age"`, `"Mixed Case"`, `"with space"`, `"uni☃"` | Work, stored verbatim (delimited identifiers are case-preserving) |
   169	| SQL `"person/name"` or `"a/b"` | Lexer error (`token recognition error at: '"person/'`). `/` cannot appear in a SQL identifier |
   170	| XTQL `(from :nsy [_id person/name])` | Reads column `person$name`. `person.name2` reads `person$name2` |
   171	| XTQL `person/age` against a SQL-created `"person.age"` | NULL, because XTQL normalises to `person$age` |
   172	| XTQL `[firstName]` | Reads `firstname`. `[first-name]` reads `first_name` |
   173	| `acme.person` in SQL and `:acme/person` in XTQL | Same table (schema `acme`) |
   174	| `INSERT INTO PersonRecord` | Table `personrecord` |
   175	| XTQL `[xt/valid-from xt/system-from]` | Resolve to `_valid_from`, `_system_from` |
   176	
   177	- Collisions are silent. `first-name` and `first_name` are the same XTQL column. `FirstName` and `firstname` are the same XTQL column. Two SQL delimited names that differ only in case coexist as separate columns (`"Name"` and `name` both existed in probe p3d), yet XTQL can only reach the lowercase one, and `SELECT *` shows both.
   178	- Unquoted SQL identifiers that are reserved: in a 41-word probe only `order`, `group`, `default`, `from`, `to` were rejected unquoted. `name`, `date`, `text`, `year`, `user`, `position`, `first`, `last`, `period`, `row`, `rank`, `day`, `time`, `timestamp`, `uuid`, `value`, `key`, `version` were accepted. Upstream #6014 (broader reserved list in rc1) is fixed in the 2.2.0 image.
   179	- Unknown names: since 2.2 SQL errors on an unknown table or column ("Table not found", "Column not found"). XTQL returns `NULL` for an unknown column and zero rows for an unknown table. `CREATE [OR ALTER] TABLE t (_id, name, email)` (v2.2+, `docs/.../sql/schema.md`) pre-declares columns of type `:nothing` so SQL `SELECT` works before the first write (probe p2b).
   180	- Introspection: `information_schema.columns` returns `data_type` (Postgres name) and `xt_type` (XTDB type notation).
   181	
   182	**Evidence: types as seen by the `postgres` 3.4.9 driver** (probe p3c, one query per column; OIDs from `PgType.kt`):
   183	
   184	| XTDB type | OID | JS value from `postgres` | Notes |
   185	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   186	| BIGINT (`:i64`) | 20 | string | `9223372036854775807` round-trips as a string |
   187	| INT, SMALLINT | 23, 21 | number | |
   188	| DOUBLE | 701 | number | |
   189	| REAL | 700 | number | |
   190	| DECIMAL(p,s) | 1700 | string | `12.34` and `12345678901234567890.123456789` exact |
   191	| BOOLEAN | 16 | boolean | |
   192	| TEXT | 25 | string | Unicode round-trips |
   193	| UUID | 2950 | string | |
   194	| DATE | 1082 | `Date` (UTC midnight) | |
   195	| TIMESTAMP (no zone) | 1114 | `Date` parsed in the **local** zone | Shifted by 5 hours on this machine. Avoid |
   196	| TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE | 1184 | `Date` | Millisecond precision in JS. `...123456Z` arrives as `.123Z`. Use `CAST(x AS VARCHAR)` for microseconds |
   197	| TIME | 1083 | string | |
   198	| DURATION | 11112 (custom) | string `PT1H30M` | `PgType.kt:524-527` |
   199	| INTERVAL | 1186 | string `P14M` | |
   200	| KEYWORD | 11111 (custom) | string without colon (`person/name`) | `PgType.kt:764-767` |
   201	| VARBINARY | 17 | `Buffer` | |
   202	| list of BIGINT | 1016 | array of strings | |
   203	| list of TEXT | 1009 | array of strings | |
   204	| list of DOUBLE, mixed list, empty list | 114 (JSON) | parsed JSON | `PgType.kt:995-1010` |
   205	| struct | 114 (JSON) | object | `PgType.kt:942` |
   206	| set | 114 (JSON) | array | `PgType.kt:989-990`. Set identity is lost on read |
   207	| **URI** | n/a | **`SELECT` fails**: `unknown type: java.net.URI` | `api/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/JsonSerde.kt:77`. `SELECT *` on a table with a URI column fails whole |
   208	| NULL-only column | 25 | `null` | |
   209	
   210	- **Unions.** A column's type is the join of every value ever written and only widens (`docs/.../about/types-in-xtdb.md`). A union (for example `#{:i64 :utf8}` or `#{:f64 :i64}`) is sent as OID 114 JSON. In the probe, `c_i64 = 9007199254740993` read back as `9007199254740992` after another row put text in the same column, because the value travelled as a JSON number. A double column that received one integer literal became `#{:f64 :i64}`. The repo client's rule (safe integer is OID 20, else OID 701) therefore creates unions whenever a float attribute holds a whole number. A projection must send floats with an explicit float type every time.
   211	- **Cardinality-many.** Use lists. Membership works as `'a' = ANY(tags)`, `UNNEST(tags)` in SQL, and `(unnest {:tag tags})` in XTQL (probe p4). `$1` text[] parameters work with `= ANY($1)` and `IN (SELECT x FROM UNNEST($1) AS u(x))` (probe p9b). XTQL `contains?` is the PERIOD predicate and does not test collection membership. `ARRAY_POSITION` does not exist. Sets (`[:set :utf8]`) can be written only through a transit-typed parameter or transit `COPY`, since SQL has no set literal, and they read back as JSON arrays.
   212	- **Nested structs.** SQL literal `{a: 1, b: {c: 'x'}}` or `RECORD(...)` works. From Node, parameters need the transit OID 16384 (quoted JSON transit text `["^ ","~:a",1]`). OID 114/3802 parameters become text.
   213	- **Heterogeneous rows and later columns.** Tables are sparse and schemaless. A column appears on first write, `NULL` and "missing" are the same value, and `what-is-xtdb.md:38` says tables are "always 'sparse' ... and 'wide'". Upstream #5714 (compaction failure "cannot promote DUV leg" after column presence and type changes across generations) is closed with fix commits dated to 2026-09-22, which is before the 2.2.0 tag. Inclusion in the 2.2.0 image was not verified.
   214	- **Column count.** No limit was hit. A single document with 8,001 columns inserted in 228 ms and `SELECT *` returned all 8,001 columns in 217 ms (probe p9). Upstream PR #6200 (draft, not in 2.2.0) reports that building the metadata page index costs time proportional to columns times pages, so very wide tables get slower to open.
   215	- **Fractional-ms valid time.** `postgres` serialises a `Date` parameter with millisecond precision (`toISOString`). Microseconds need a text parameter plus `CAST($1 AS TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE)`, or `TIMESTAMP '1970-01-01T00:00:00Z' + DURATION 'PT1700000000.123456S'` (probe p10: `2023-11-14T22:13:20.123456Z`). Float-multiply on `INTERVAL` is not supported ("* not applicable to types f64 and interval").
   216	
   217	**Confidence.** High for everything probed. Medium for column-count behaviour beyond 8,001 and for the 2.2.0 status of #5714.
   218	
   219	**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
   220	
   221	- Choose physical column names deliberately. `person$name` (namespace kept) cannot collide across entity types and matches both `person/name` in XTQL and `person$name` in SQL. A bare local name (`name`) reads best in raw XTQL (`(from :person [name email])`) but collides across namespaces.
   222	- Because normalisation lowercases and folds `-` to `_`, two Triplex attributes that differ only in case or `-` versus `_` would share one column. The projection needs a collision check at schema deploy time.
   223	- Pin every attribute to one XTDB type at the write boundary (float attribute: always float8, integer attribute: always int8 or always decimal) to keep columns monomorphic.
   224	- Use text-typed ISO strings or `CAST` for timestamps that need microseconds. Avoid TIMESTAMP without zone, URI and sets.
   225	- Pre-declare columns with `CREATE OR ALTER TABLE person (...)` so SQL users never hit "Column not found" on an attribute nobody has written yet.
   226	
