    39	## Q1. Can a client set a transaction's system time over pgwire in 2.2.0?
    40	
    41	**Verdict.** Yes. Four spellings work. The value must be strictly later than the latest completed transaction's system time, the resolution is one microsecond, and a violation aborts the transaction and moves the "latest completed" head forward. Mapping Triplex `recordedAt` onto native system time is possible only if nothing else ever writes to that database and every abort is accounted for.
    42	
    43	**Evidence.**
    44	
    45	*Syntax that worked (all probed, all committed on a fresh node):*
    46	
    47	```sql
    48	BEGIN READ WRITE WITH (SYSTEM_TIME = TIMESTAMP '2020-01-01T00:00:00Z');   -- grammar: Sql.g4:884-899
    49	BEGIN READ WRITE, SYSTEM_TIME = TIMESTAMP '2020-01-03T00:00:00Z';
    50	BEGIN; SET TRANSACTION READ WRITE, SYSTEM_TIME = TIMESTAMP '2020-01-02T00:00:00Z';
    51	BEGIN READ WRITE WITH (SYSTEM_TIME = '2020-01-06T00:00:00Z');             -- plain string literal
    52	```
    53	
    54	- Grammar: `core/src/main/antlr/xtdb/antlr/Sql.g4:898` (`'SYSTEM_TIME' '='? txOptionValue # SystemTimeTxOption`).
    55	- Docs: `docs/src/content/docs/reference/main/sql/txs.md:222-226`. Strictly later, each backfill tx gets its own time, a non-advancing time aborts the tx, and the abort is recorded in `xt.txs`.
    56	- The JVM API spelling is `:system-time` (`docs/src/content/docs/reference/main/xtql/txs.md`, "Transaction options"). Not usable from Node.
    57	
    58	*Constraint rules in source:*
    59	
    60	- `core/src/main/kotlin/xtdb/indexer/TxResolver.kt:211-213`. With no explicit time the tx takes the log message timestamp, bumped to latest-completed plus 1 microsecond if that is not later. So even default system times are strictly increasing and no two transactions share one.
    61	- `TxResolver.kt:215-229`. An explicit time that is equal to or earlier than the latest completed tx aborts with `invalid-system-time` / "specified system-time must be later than current tx". The comment cites issue #6086 (equal times make a read basis ambiguous).
    62	- `TxResolver.kt:231`. The aborted transaction is recorded with the default (smoothed) time, so it still advances the head.
    63	- `TxResolver.kt:235-246`. The effective system time also becomes the tx's `currentTime`, which is the default `_valid_from` and `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`.
    64	- Release note for v2.2.0-beta3 fixes #6086: before that, equal times were accepted. In the 2.2.0 image they are rejected (probe below).
    65	
    66	*Probes:*
    67	
    68	| Probe | Result |
    69	| --- | --- |
    70	| Times `2020-01-01T00:00:00Z`, `...00.000001Z`, `...00.000002123Z` in sequence | All committed. The nanosecond literal was truncated to `...00.000002Z` (stored `system_time` `2020-01-01T00:00:00.000002Z`), not rejected |
    71	| Offset literal `2020-01-05T01:00:00+02:00` | Stored as `2020-01-04T23:00Z` |
    72	| Equal to latest | `COMMIT SYNC` fails, SQLSTATE `08P01`, message `specified system-time must be later than current tx` |
    73	| Earlier than latest | Same error |
    74	| `ASYNC = true` plus a bad time | `COMMIT` returns normally. The abort appears later in `xt.txs` |
    75	| Far future (`2099-01-01T00:00:00Z`) | Accepted. The next default-time transactions took `2099-01-01T00:00:00.000001Z`, `...000002Z` (head + 1 microsecond) |
    76	| Rows written at a far-future system time | Invisible to default queries, because default valid time is `now` and the default `_valid_from` equals that future system time. `SELECT _id FROM t1` returned 8 of 11 rows. `xt.txs` also hid its own future-dated rows until `FOR ALL VALID_TIME` |
    77	| Three consecutive bad times at the head `2099-...000002Z` | Each abort took the next microsecond (`...000003Z`, `...000004Z`, `...000005Z`), so a retry must advance past the abort, not past the time it asked for |
    78	| **Past-dated backfill, then one bad time** | The abort took wall-clock now (`2026-11-07T14:39:14.336Z`). Every later explicit past time (2019, then `2020-01-01T00:00:00.000001Z`, `...000002123Z`, `2020-01-02`) was rejected, including ones that would have been valid before the abort |
    79	
    80	Raw output: Appendix B1.
    81	
    82	*Anything else that moves the head:* upstream issue #6209 (open) lists DDL-only transactions (`CREATE TABLE`, `GRANT`, `REVOKE`), aborted transactions, empty transactions and `ATTACH/DETACH DATABASE`. Its text says "In 2.2 a DDL or abort during a `SYSTEM_TIME` backfill still moves the clock past the next step." The proposed fix PR #6212 was closed unmerged on 2026-10-19. No release contains a fix.
    83	
    84	**Confidence.** High. Source, docs, upstream issue and probes agree.
    85	
    86	**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
    87	
    88	```
    89	   Triplex clock (ms, ties allowed)         Native system time (us, strictly increasing)
    90	   -----------------------------------      ---------------------------------------------
    91	   recordedAt  = 1700000000123              head = max over ALL txs incl. aborts and DDL
    92	   recordedAt  = 1700000000123  (tie!)      next explicit time must be > head
    93	   CAS conflict -> abort                     abort takes head+1us or wall-clock now
    94	```
    95	
    96	- A one-to-one mapping `recordedAt -> system_time` breaks on ties. A ledger position can be mapped to a strictly increasing value (for example epoch microseconds plus a position offset), but every `triplex-cas-conflict` abort advances the head to wall-clock now or past it. After that, an explicit time derived from a logical clock that is behind wall-clock fails permanently.
    97	- Safe shapes, in order of fit:
    98	  1. **Engine-assigned system time** (no `SYSTEM_TIME` option). Keep `recorded_position` and `recorded_at` as Triplex ledger columns. Record the engine time per commit by writing `METADATA = {op_id: ...}` and reading `xt.txs.system_time` by `user_metadata->>'op_id'` (probe in Q6). Translate Triplex `recordedAt/position` read bases to a snapshot token or a `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` value through the ledger.
    99	  2. **Client-set system time** only if Triplex becomes the sole writer of that database, never runs DDL or grants after start, and re-reads `max(system_time)` from `xt.txs` after every abort before choosing the next value.
   100	- Native `_system_from/_system_to` on projection rows then mean "engine time of the projection write". They will not equal Triplex `recordedAt` unless option 2 is accepted.
