   275	## Q5. Temporal reads, snapshots and basis tokens
   276	
   277	**Verdict.** The read surface is complete and behaves as documented: valid-time and system-time filters in SQL and XTQL, per-query and per-transaction basis, snapshot tokens for repeatable cuts, await tokens for read-your-writes.
   278	
   279	**Evidence.**
   280	
   281	*Syntax.* `FOR VALID_TIME AS OF t | FROM a TO b | BETWEEN a AND b | ALL`, the same for `SYSTEM_TIME`, `FOR ALL VALID_TIME`, and `FOR VALID_TIME ONLY FROM a TO b` (v2.2+, clamps returned bounds to the window, `queries.md:186-205`). XTQL: `:for-valid-time` / `:for-system-time` with `(at t)`, `(in a b)`, `(from t)`, `(to t)` or `:all-time` (`xtql.clj:233-262`). The `#inst` reader literal inside XTQL text has millisecond precision. Use a typed `timestamptz` parameter or SQL for microseconds.
   282	
   283	*Defaults.* System time is "as best known" (latest processed tx on the node). Valid time is "as of now" where now is `CLOCK_TIME` if set, else the node clock (`queries.md` precedence list). A row whose `_valid_from` is in the future is invisible by default (Q1 probe).
   284	
   285	*Probe p5 (three transactions: A inserted 2024-01-01 onwards, update from 2024-06-01 to B, retroactive X over March):*
   286	
   287	| Query | Result | Reading |
   288	| --- | --- | --- |
   289	| default | `B` | now |
   290	| `FOR VALID_TIME AS OF 2024-03-15` | `X` | corrected history |
   291	| `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t2 ... FOR VALID_TIME AS OF 2024-03-15` | `A` | what we believed before the retroactive correction |
   292	| `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF t3 ... FOR VALID_TIME AS OF 2024-03-15` | `X` | after it |
   293	| `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF 2000-01-01 FOR ALL VALID_TIME` | empty | before first tx |
   294	| `FOR ALL SYSTEM_TIME FOR ALL VALID_TIME` | 6 rows with exact `_system_from/_system_to` | full history |
   295	| `SETTING SNAPSHOT_TIME = t2` | rows as of t2 | approximate cut |
   296	| `SETTING SNAPSHOT_TOKEN = tok1` | only the first tx's row | exact cut |
   297	
   298	*Basis and tokens.*
   299	
   300	- `SHOW SNAPSHOT_TOKEN`, `SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN`, `SHOW CLOCK_TIME` exist. Tokens are short base64 protobuf strings (`ChYKBHh0ZGISDgoMCJn0vNcGEJCGkakB`, `Cg0KBHh0ZGISBQoDnv0D`).
   301	- `SETTING SNAPSHOT_TOKEN = '...', CLOCK_TIME = TIMESTAMP '...' <query>` is a per-query basis. The repo client already emits exactly this.
   302	- `BEGIN READ ONLY WITH (SNAPSHOT_TOKEN = ..., CLOCK_TIME = ..., AWAIT_TOKEN = ...)` fixes the basis for several statements. Probe: inside `BEGIN READ ONLY`, a second `COUNT(*)` after another connection committed a write returned the same count (6), and the next statement outside the transaction saw the write. `queries.md:7-14` and `txs-in-xtdb.md:85` say the basis is fixed at the first query (a 2.2 change).
   303	- Read-your-writes on one connection is automatic. Across connections, `SET AWAIT_TOKEN = '<token>'` where the token came from `SHOW AWAIT_TOKEN` after commit. In the probe the second connection already saw the write without it, which the docs say is likely but not guaranteed (`txs-in-xtdb.md:40-66`).
   304	- `SNAPSHOT_TIME` is only an approximation if the node is behind (`txs-in-xtdb.md:136-141`). For audit-grade repeatability store the token and the clock time.
   305	- Transaction ids are log positions, not dense counters (`xt.txs._id` values `0, 1555, 3110, ...` on the local log).
   306	
   307	**Confidence.** High.
   308	
   309	**Implication for the entity-projection design.**
   310	
   311	- A Triplex read basis `{recordedAt, validAt}` can be answered natively as `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF <engine time of the commit at that position> FOR VALID_TIME AS OF <validAt>`, but only if the position-to-engine-time mapping is stored (Q1 option 1).
   312	- A fixed-cut repeatable page (cursor) maps to a stored `SNAPSHOT_TOKEN` plus `CLOCK_TIME`. Tokens are engine-specific, so Triplex's own `recorded_position` remains the portable cut.
   313	- Always pass `validAt` explicitly, because the engine default is wall-clock now.
   314	
