   321	## 4. Recommended physical naming
   322	
   323	### 4.1 Rules for both engines
   324	
   325	1. **Names derive from schema keys.** Attribute keys and entity type names are the inputs. TypeScript aliases never are, because renaming an alias must not rename stored data.
   326	2. **One pure function per engine computes all names from a release.** `PhysicalSchema.compile(release)` follows the `ConfigApi` pattern: an override map, default derivation, and a hard failure on any collision that names both inputs.
   327	3. **The same attribute has the same physical name everywhere.** `:common/created-by` is `common$created_by` in every entity relation that uses it. Requiredness and cardinality are properties of the usage, not of the name.
   328	4. **Time and visibility are never part of a stored name.** They come from the basis-closed fragments (`facts()`, `entity()`, `values()`, `visible()`).
   329	5. **Layouts are versioned.** A new layout gets a new mapping id in the trace (for example `triplex-xtdb/entity-shaped/2`), so old traces stay interpretable.
   330	6. **Reserved names are never produced.** Anything beginning with `_`, `xt`, `tx_`, `pg_`, or equal to `information_schema`. The same check runs for RDF prefix labels (4.3.2).
   331	
   332	### 4.2 XTDB
   333	
   334	#### 4.2.1 Layer 1: canonical (unchanged)
   335	
   336	Keep `tx_assertions` as it is. What changes is documentation of the contract raw authors may rely on.
   337	
   338	| Column | Meaning |
   339	|--------|---------|
   340	| `id` | Assertion id (`TripleId`). The XTDB `_id` is `["scope","id"]` as JSON and is not part of the contract. |
   341	| `scope_key` | Tenant. Every raw query must filter it, and `facts()` does. |
   342	| `entity_id`, `entity_type` | Entity id string and the type tag on the assertion. |
   343	| `attribute` | The verbatim key `':person/name'`, so the TypeScript key equals the stored value and is greppable. |
   344	| `value_type` plus `value_string/number/boolean/datetime/json` | Typed carrier. Normally one carrier is non-null (`int64` is a decimal string in `value_string`; a blob uses `value_string` and `value_json`). |
   345	| `valid_from`, `valid_to`, `recorded_at`, `recorded_position`, `retracted_at`, `retracted_position`, `tx_id`, `retract_tx_id` | Triplex time and journal coordinates. They are Triplex's, not XTDB's temporal columns. Every insert pins XTDB's native `_valid_from` to the epoch so a replaced row replaces its whole native lifetime `[repo XtdbStorageAdapter.ts:31-40]`. |
   346	
   347	#### 4.2.2 Layer 2: three generated relations
   348	
   349	| Fragment | Shape | Use |
   350	|----------|-------|-----|
   351	| `facts()` (exists) | One row per assertion visible at the basis, with the Layer 1 columns minus `scope_key`. | Anything about assertion identity or intervals. |
   352	| `entity(Person)` (new) | One row per entity that has a `Person` assertion visible at the basis. Columns: `_id` plus one column per attribute the type uses. | Reading and filtering whole entities. |
   353	| `values(Person.attributes.skills)` (new) | One row per visible value of one attribute. Columns: `_id`, `value`, `assertion`, `valid_from`, `valid_to`, `recorded_position`. | Joins on refs, many-valued attributes, interval questions for one attribute. |
   354	
   355	All three are subquery sources, so the SDK renders them in place. The trace records the expanded text, which is how a reader who knows only SQL sees what ran.
   356	
   357	#### 4.2.3 Name derivation
   358	
   359	| Input | Output | Rule | Checked in `[ran]` |
   360	|-------|--------|------|--------------------|
   361	| `:person/name` | `person$name` | Strip `:`, apply XTDB normal form | yes |
   362	| `:person/first-name` | `person$first_name` | `-` becomes `_` | yes |
   363	| `:placement/worker` | `placement$worker` | same | yes |
   364	| `:common/created-by` | `common$created_by` | same | yes |
   365	| `Person` | `person` | PascalCase to snake_case, acronym aware | yes |
   366	| `ProjectMember` | `project_member` | XTDB's own normal form alone would give `projectmember`, so Triplex snake-cases first | yes |
   367	| `HTTPRoute` | `http_route` | acronym run splits before the last capital | yes |
   368	| `W2Form` | `w2_form` | digit then capital splits | yes |
   369	| entity id | `_id`, unchanged | The raw `EntityId` string | n/a |
   370	
   371	**Collisions the compile must reject.** Both were reproduced in `[ran]` (`lab/research/R3-artifacts/physical-names.mjs`).
   372	
   373	- Hyphen and underscore are equivalent under XTDB normal form, and the Triplex key regex allows both. `:person/first-name` and `:person/first_name` both become `person$first_name`. `:a-b/c` and `:a_b/c` both become `a_b$c`.
   374	- Two entity type names can snake-case to one name: `ABTest` and `AbTest` both become `ab_test`.
   375	
   376	**Relation names that are SQL reserved words.** `Order` becomes `order`. Generated text always quotes relation aliases, and raw authors choose their own alias (`FROM ${entity(Order)} o`).
