   421	### 4.3 RDF (QLever)
   422	
   423	#### 4.3.1 IRI scheme
   424	
   425	| Thing | Default IRI | Notes |
   426	|-------|-------------|-------|
   427	| Attribute `:person/first-name` | `urn:triplex:attr:person:first-name` | prefix label `person:` bound to `urn:triplex:attr:person:`, so SPARQL reads `person:first-name`. Hyphen, underscore and digits are legal in a SPARQL prefixed-name local part, so no escaping is ever needed. A `/` would need `\/`, which is why the namespace and name are split at the prefix. |
   428	| Entity type `Person` | `urn:triplex:type:Person` | prefix `type:` |
   429	| Entity id `person:maria` | `urn:triplex:entity:person:maria` | prefix `entity:` and the encoding in 4.3.3 |
   430	| Assertion | `urn:triplex:assertion:<b64url(id)>` | exists, `[repo rdf.ts:47]` |
   431	| Scope graph | `urn:triplex:scope:<b64url(scope)>` | exists, `[repo rdf.ts:46]` |
   432	| Engine fields | `urn:triplex:field:<column>` | exists. Treat as the private engine channel, `[repo rdf.ts:50]`. |
   433	| Optional http scheme | `{base}attr/person/first-name`, `{base}type/Person`, `{base}entity/person:maria` | for publishing linked data. Same functions, different base. |
   434	
   435	**Prefix labels are presentation.** The IRIs are the contract. The generated prologue binds `type`, `entity`, `assertion`, `field`, `rdf`, `xsd` for fixed kinds. If an attribute namespace equals one of those (for example `:entity/name`), its label becomes `entity_`. A derived prologue is a convenience; results never depend on a label.
   436	
   437	#### 4.3.2 Typed literals
   438	
   439	| Triplex type | RDF object | Exactness |
   440	|--------------|------------|-----------|
   441	| `text`, `enumOf` | plain `xsd:string` literal | exact |
   442	| `date` | `"2026-01-01"^^xsd:date` | exact |
   443	| `instant` | `"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime` | exact to the lexical form. Sub-millisecond fractions are in the lexical form; QLever's inline `dateTime` precision is `[unverified]` (P5). |
   444	| `boolean` | `xsd:boolean` | exact |
   445	| `number` | `xsd:double` | QLever keeps doubles in 60 bits, so a double whose low four mantissa bits are non-zero is not exact natively. The repo already measures this: `numericShadowIsExact` is true only when `(low32 & 15) === 0` `[repo rdf.ts:83-89]`. Exact comparison uses the existing `_order` channel. |
   446	| `integer` | `xsd:integer` | exact for safe integers |
   447	| `int64` | `"9223372036854775807"^^xsd:long` | exact in the lexical form. QLever parses `xsd:long` as an integer datatype `[clone references/qlever/src/parser/RdfParser.h:165-177]` and encodes natively only within about ±2^59 (the repo's limit, `[repo rdf.ts:249]`). Behaviour beyond that is P5. |
   448	| `ref` | the target entity IRI | exact |
   449	| `json` | `"..."^^rdf:JSON` (the datatype JSON-LD 1.1 defines) with the canonical JSON text already produced by `canonicalJson` | exact |
   450	| `blob` | not published as a plain triple | use the assertion node |
   451	
   452	#### 4.3.3 Entity IRI encoding
   453	
   454	Entity ids are arbitrary strings, so the encoding must be injective and canonical. The rule, verified in `[ran]` (`physical-names.mjs`: round trip on 14 representative ids, injectivity over all 7,380 strings up to length 4 over a 9-symbol alphabet including `%`, `/`, `.`, `-`, `:`, space and a non-ASCII letter, rejection of the empty string and of lone surrogates):
   455	
   456	1. Reject empty ids and ids that are not well-formed Unicode (the repo already requires this for QLever text [repo rdf.ts:38-44]).
   457	2. Encode the id as UTF-8 and keep only `A-Z a-z 0-9 _ :`, plus `-` when not first and `.` when neither first nor last. Encode every other byte as `%` plus two **uppercase** hex digits. Never encode a kept character.
   458	3. The decoder accepts only canonical forms (uppercase hex, no encoded safe characters), so one id has exactly one IRI.
   459	
   460	| Id | IRI segment |
   461	|----|-------------|
   462	| `worker:maria` | `worker:maria` |
   463	| `case:01J9XYZABC` | `case:01J9XYZABC` |
   464	| `a/b c` | `a%2Fb%20c` |
   465	| `100%` | `100%25` |
   466	| `é/ü` | `%C3%A9%2F%C3%BC` |
   467	| `-lead` | `%2Dlead` |
   468	| `trail.` | `trail%2E` |
   469	| `.` and `..` | `%2E` and `%2E%2E` |
   470	| `x~y` | `x%7Ey` |
   471	| `emoji:😀` | `emoji:%F0%9F%98%80` |
   472	
   473	The kept set is chosen so the segment is also a legal local part in a prefixed name (`entity:worker:maria`) without backslash escapes, and the result is never a dot segment. `encodeURIComponent` is not usable here because it leaves `~ ! ' ( ) *` unencoded, which breaks the prefixed-name rule for `~` and quoting for `'`.
