   117	4. **Hypothesis H-X-CUT (exact recorded cut without setting system time).** Every row written in
   118	   one XTDB transaction shares that transaction's `_system_from`, and system time strictly increases
   119	   per transaction. So commit position P maps exactly to the `_system_from` of the `tx_meta` row
   120	   version written in P's transaction, and a Triplex read at recorded cut P can run as
   121	   `FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF` that instant. This must be proven, including after aborts, restarts and
   122	   same-millisecond commits, before any read depends on it.
   123	5. **Types.** Each projection column has exactly one declared type; int64 is always BIGINT;
   124	   mixed-type columns are forbidden because they reach Node as JSON and lose integer precision.
   125	   Valid-time coordinates that cannot be represented exactly in microseconds route that entity's
   126	   reads to the ledger.
   127	6. **Card-one conflicts.** When the ledger holds two live, overlapping values for a cardinality-one
   128	   attribute (possible when enforcement is off), the projection writes NULL and records the attribute
   129	   in a `_triplex_conflicts` column; the repository surfaces a typed conflict error. Raw queries see
   130	   the NULL and the marker, never an arbitrary pick.
   131	7. **Engine bugs to route around.** `SUM` over BIGINT wraps silently; XTQL `union-all` cannot be
   132	   planned; window functions hit `AbstractMethodError`. Native helpers reject or rewrite these shapes.
   133	
   134	### 3.3 Lane Q physical design
   135	
   136	1. **Compact ledger.** Assertion nodes shrink from about 30 triples to about 7: entity, attribute,
   137	   value (exact lexical form), valid-from key, optional valid-to key, transaction node, plus a text
   138	   order key where the attribute is orderable. Recorded time, actor and command live once on the
   139	   transaction node. The journal is derived from assertion nodes (created or retracted per position)
   140	   instead of stored change JSON, if R9 confirms core can accept a native journal.
   141	2. **Current graph.** Plain triples (`rdf:type`, attribute predicates, typed literals only inside the
   142	   engine's exact ranges) for facts live at the newest position and valid at the graph's declared
   143	   valid instant. Plain attribute-predicate triples exist nowhere else, so raw queries over QLever's
   144	   union default graph never see historical values. A boundary maintainer applies due changes when
   145	   validity starts or ends; every read reports the graph's position and valid instant.
   146	3. **Rival H-Q3b.** Plain triples inside per-assertion named graphs (R3's preference, exact at any
   147	   basis). Kept as a measured rival because 100m named graphs are an unmeasured cost and the union
   148	   default graph would expose historical values to raw queries.
   149	4. **Roll-forward write protocol.** One operation per request; ledger triples applied idempotently;
   150	   read-back verification; a position watermark published only after verification; readers bound
   151	   every ledger read by the watermark; a reconciler re-applies positions above the watermark after a
   152	   crash; the projection follows its own watermark. Delta is bounded by online `rebuild-index`
   153	   (first trigger near 1M delta triples). R8 measures each step.
   154	5. **Semantic fixes.** Text order keys (R5, proven). `ctx.notExists`/`ctx.exists` helpers that
   155	   re-bind correlated variables plus a static check (R5, verified). `query-planning-budget` set low
   187	**Shared work stays thin.** The repository types and descriptors (engine independent, a new core
   188	subpath), the projection spec compiler (names, types, collision checks), and the fixture generator.
   189	
   190	**The oracle is the in-memory KV runtime.** Triplex's KV backend is a supported reference
   191	implementation. The entity suite applies one command stream to the KV runtime and to a lane engine,
   192	then compares reads at the same bases. Native task oracles stay the independent ones already in
   193	`qualification/workloads` (`event_oracles.py`).
   218	1. **Read state.** `lab/STATE.md`, `lab/SCORECARD.md`, and the tail of `lab/NOTEBOOK.md`.
   219	2. **Select.** Per lane, pick the lowest-scoring goal that has a feasible next experiment; keep two or
   220	   three rival hypotheses alive for that bottleneck.
   221	3. **Brief and dispatch.** Each experiment gets `lab/experiments/EXP-NNN-slug/BRIEF.md`.
   222	4. **Build and run.** Coding agents work in their own git worktree on `exp/NNN-slug` and run gates
   223	   through `lab/bin`.
   224	5. **Verify.** A different agent reruns the gates on the branch and tries to break the claim.
   225	6. **Select.** Promote to `main` only when the target gate improves and no lane gate regresses
   226	   against the last `main` receipt. Losers stay in the notebook with the reason.
   227	7. **Next generation.** Mutate winners, combine partial winners, retire hypotheses that failed twice
   228	   for the same reason.
   254	### 5.4 Gates and receipts
   255	
   256	Every gate writes a JSON receipt to `lab/runs/<gate>/<run-id>.json` (small, committed) and keeps raw
   257	output under `runs/` (ignored by git, kept on disk).
   258	
   259	| Gate | What it proves | Needs engine |
   260	| --- | --- | --- |
   261	| G0 | typecheck and pure unit tests for core, xtdb, qlever, qualification | no |
   262	| G1 | unchanged44 shared conformance, current and reopen | yes |
   263	| G2 | native142 (71 tasks, current and reopen); lane X tallies XTQL and SQL separately | yes |
   264	| G3 | entity suite: repository and raw queries against the KV oracle; projection equals its ledger function | yes |
   265	| G4 | semantic counterexamples with original expected answers | yes |
   266	| G5 | write semantics: duplicate command, CAS conflict, unknown outcome, crash and reopen | yes |
   267	| G6 | scale rung (10k, 100k, 1m, 10m) | yes, scale window |
   268	| G7 | load: 64, 256, then 512 and 1024 clients with steady writes | yes, scale window |
   269	
   270	**Receipt fields:** gate, lane, git SHA and dirty flag, engine image digest, engine config hash,
   271	fixture id and seed, start and end time, pass and fail counts, first failures (kept, never
   272	overwritten), timings, and resource observations (peak container memory and CPU, volume bytes).
   299	## 7. Resource envelope
   300	
   301	| Resource | Envelope | Rule |
   302	| --- | --- | --- |
   303	| Docker VM | colima profile `triplex2`: 6 CPU, 12 GiB, 60 GiB disk | always `DOCKER_HOST=unix:///Users/sam/.colima/triplex2/docker.sock`; after any colima command run `docker context use colima` |
   304	| Resident dev engines | `tx2-xtdb-dev` (about 3 GiB), `tx2-qlever-dev` (about 2 GiB) | correctness work shares them through disjoint database scopes |
   305	| Heavy slots | 3 slots, about 3.5 GiB each, own ports and named volumes | performance, destructive and crash tests |
   306	| Scale window | one at a time; stops other slots | rungs 1m and 10m, load tests |
   307	| Disk | VM 60 GiB hard; stop new heavy runs at 50 GiB used; repo plus references under 5 GB | measured per receipt |
   308	| Agents | at most 10 concurrent; at most 4 concurrent heavy local test or typecheck runs | `lab/bin/heavy` lock |
   309	
   310	The VM can grow (Sam's Mac has 36 GiB RAM and about 93 GB free disk) when a rung measures the need;
   311	the change and its justification go in the notebook. Sam's default Docker VM is never touched.
